window.REVIEW_BANK = {"drill":{"name":"Plants, Fungi & Signals","key":"bio-u3-progress-v1","html":true,"href":"../drill/index.html","items":{"HW0":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q1","q":"What are the closest living relatives of land plants?","ok":"Charophytes (a group of green algae)","wrong":["Red algae (rhodophytes)","Brown algae (phaeophytes)","Diatoms (bacillariophytes)"],"ex":"Charophytes are the green algae most closely related to land plants. Both DNA comparisons and shared cell features (cellulose-making rosettes, sperm structure, sporopollenin) point to them.","img":null},"HW1":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q2 (advantages)","q":"When plants first colonized land, which conditions were advantages?","ok":"Unfiltered sunlight, more CO<sub>2</sub>, nutrient-rich soil, and at first few herbivores or pathogens","wrong":["Water all around the body, so there was no risk of drying out","The buoyancy of water to hold the body up","An easy route for swimming sperm to reach eggs"],"ex":"On land, light isn't filtered by water, CO<sub>2</sub> is more plentiful in air, soil holds minerals, and early on there were almost no animals to eat plants or microbes to infect them.","img":null},"HW2":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q2 (problems)","q":"Which were the main problems early land plants had to overcome?","ok":"Drying out (scarce water) and no water to support the body against gravity","wrong":["Too little sunlight for photosynthesis","Too little CO<sub>2</sub> in the air","Constant attack by herbivores from the start"],"ex":"Out of the water, plants lose water to the air and have nothing buoyant to hold them up. Adaptations like the cuticle, stomata, vascular tissue and lignin solve these problems.","img":null},"HW3":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q3 (shared traits)","q":"Which traits do land plants share with charophytes?","ok":"Ring-shaped (rosette) cellulose-synthesizing complexes, similar flagellated sperm, and sporopollenin that protects the zygote from drying","wrong":["Vascular tissue, stomata and a waxy cuticle","Alternation of generations with a dominant sporophyte","Seeds, pollen and flowers"],"ex":"These three features are found only in land plants and charophytes. Sporopollenin, a tough polymer, keeps charophyte zygotes from drying out at the edges of ponds. Its use in spore walls is part of what let plants move onto land.","img":null},"HW4":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q3 (what's different)","q":"Which traits distinguish land plants (embryophytes) from charophytes?","ok":"Alternation of generations with dependent embryos, walled spores made in sporangia, multicellular gametangia, and apical meristems","wrong":["Chlorophyll <i>a</i> and <i>b</i>, cellulose cell walls and starch storage","Flagellated sperm and sporopollenin","Rosette cellulose-synthesizing complexes"],"ex":"These are the derived traits of land plants. Chlorophyll <i>a</i> and <i>b</i>, cellulose walls and starch are shared with green algae, and the other wrong answers are shared with charophytes, so none of them separate the two groups.","img":null},"HW5":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q4 (classified by)","q":"Plants can be classified based on the presence or absence of","ok":"vascular tissue","wrong":["chlorophyll","cell walls","alternation of generations"],"ex":"Vascular tissue (xylem and phloem) divides plants into nonvascular plants (bryophytes) and vascular plants. Every land plant has chlorophyll, cell walls and alternation of generations.","img":null},"HW6":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q4 (nonvascular)","q":"Nonvascular plants are called","ok":"bryophytes (liverworts, mosses and hornworts)","wrong":["lycophytes","pterophytes (ferns)","gymnosperms"],"ex":"Bryophytes are the nonvascular plants. Lycophytes and ferns are seedless vascular plants, and gymnosperms are seed plants.","img":null},"HW7":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q5","q":"Which generation dominates the life cycle of nonvascular plants, and which dominates in vascular plants?","ok":"Haploid gametophyte in nonvascular plants; diploid sporophyte in vascular plants","wrong":["Diploid sporophyte in nonvascular plants; haploid gametophyte in vascular plants","Sporophyte in both","Gametophyte in both"],"ex":"In a moss, the green leafy plant you see is the gametophyte (n), and the small sporophyte (2n) grows from it and depends on it. In ferns, conifers and flowering plants, the large plant is the sporophyte.","img":["../drill/img/moss-cycle.jpg","The moss life cycle: haploid gametophytes (n) and the diploid sporophyte (2n) growing from them"]},"HW8":{"subject":"Seedless vascular","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q6","q":"Which set of characteristics is specific to vascular plants?","ok":"A dominant sporophyte, xylem and phloem reinforced with lignin, and well-developed roots and leaves","wrong":["A dominant gametophyte and flagellated sperm","Seeds and flowers","Stomata and a cuticle"],"ex":"Vascular plants have life cycles dominated by the sporophyte, transport tissue (xylem and phloem), and true roots and leaves. Seeds and flowers appear only in seed plants. Bryophytes also have a cuticle, and mosses and hornworts have stomata.","img":null},"HW9":{"subject":"Seedless vascular","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q6 (xylem & phloem)","q":"In vascular tissue, what do xylem and phloem each carry?","ok":"Xylem carries water and minerals (through dead, lignified cells); phloem carries sugars and other organic products (through living cells)","wrong":["Xylem carries sugars; phloem carries water and minerals","Both carry only water","Xylem carries gametes; phloem carries spores"],"ex":"Xylem's tracheids are dead cells with lignin-reinforced walls, which also hold the plant up. Phloem is living tissue that distributes sugars, amino acids and other organic products.","img":null},"HW10":{"subject":"Seedless vascular","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q6 (height)","q":"What did lignified vascular tissue allow vascular plants to do that bryophytes cannot?","ok":"Grow tall, since it gives structural support and moves water over long distances","wrong":["Reproduce by spores","Make chlorophyll","Live without stomata"],"ex":"Lignin stiffens xylem walls, so stems could hold plants up and carry water high. Taller plants win the competition for light and spread their spores farther. This matches a clicker question in Ch 29.","img":null},"HW11":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q7 (nonvascular gametophyte)","q":"Describe the gametophyte of a nonvascular plant such as a moss.","ok":"It is the large, green, long-lived photosynthetic plant; the sporophyte grows from it and depends on it for food","wrong":["It is microscopic and lives inside the sporophyte","It is a small heart-shaped plant that dies once the sporophyte grows","It is a pollen grain carried by the wind"],"ex":"In bryophytes the gametophyte is the dominant generation, and the sporophyte stays attached to it and is nutritionally dependent on it.","img":["../drill/img/moss-cycle.jpg","The moss life cycle: haploid gametophytes (n) and the diploid sporophyte (2n) growing from them"]},"HW12":{"subject":"Seedless vascular","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q7 (seedless vascular gametophyte)","q":"Describe the gametophyte of a seedless vascular plant such as a fern.","ok":"It is small, free-living and photosynthetic, independent of the large sporophyte","wrong":["It is the large leafy plant, and the sporophyte depends on it","It is retained inside an ovule","It is a pollen grain"],"ex":"A fern gametophyte is a tiny heart-shaped plant (prothallus) on the soil. The young sporophyte starts out on it, but it soon grows independent and becomes the large fern.","img":null},"HW13":{"subject":"Seedless vascular","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q7 (fertilization)","q":"How does fertilization happen in both bryophytes and seedless vascular plants?","ok":"Flagellated sperm swim through a film of water to the egg in an archegonium","wrong":["Pollen carried by wind delivers sperm to the ovule","Two sperm travel down a pollen tube (double fertilization)","Spores fuse with eggs directly"],"ex":"Both groups depend on water for fertilization: sperm from an antheridium swim to the egg in an archegonium. That is why mosses and ferns are usually found in moist places.","img":null},"HW14":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q7 (moss spores)","q":"In a moss, which structure produces and disperses the spores?","ok":"The sporangium (capsule) at the tip of the sporophyte's stalk (seta)","wrong":["Sori on the underside of leaves","Strobili (cones)","The antheridium"],"ex":"The moss sporophyte is a foot, a stalk (seta) and a capsule (sporangium). Meiosis in the capsule makes spores, which are released when the lid comes off; the peristome teeth control how they are dispersed.","img":["../drill/img/moss-cycle.jpg","The moss life cycle: haploid gametophytes (n) and the diploid sporophyte (2n) growing from them"]},"HW15":{"subject":"Seedless vascular","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q7 (fern spores)","q":"In a fern, which structure produces and disperses the spores?","ok":"Sporangia grouped in sori on the underside of the sporophyte's leaves (sporophylls)","wrong":["A capsule on a stalk growing from the gametophyte","The archegonium","The ovule"],"ex":"Fern sporangia cluster in sori (singular: sorus) on sporophylls. In lycophytes, sporophylls are grouped into cone-like strobili. Both are on the sporophyte, the dominant generation.","img":null},"HW16":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q8 (seedless vs. seed)","q":"Which describes a key difference between seedless and seed-producing vascular plants?","ok":"Seed plants have tiny gametophytes kept inside the sporophyte and use pollen, so they don't need water for fertilization; seedless plants have free-living gametophytes and swimming sperm","wrong":["Seedless plants have no vascular tissue","Seed plants have a dominant gametophyte","Seedless plants are heterosporous and seed plants are homosporous"],"ex":"Seed plants keep their microscopic gametophytes inside the sporophyte, where they are protected from drying, and deliver sperm in pollen. Seedless vascular plants have free-living gametophytes and swimming sperm and disperse by spores.","img":null},"HW17":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q8 (pollen)","q":"What is a pollen grain?","ok":"A male gametophyte enclosed in a tough pollen wall that contains sporopollenin","wrong":["A sperm cell","A male sporophyte","A spore that grows into the female gametophyte"],"ex":"A microspore develops into a pollen grain: the male gametophyte inside a protective coat. Pollen can travel by wind or animals, so seed plants don't need water to bring sperm to eggs.","img":null},"HW18":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q8 (gametangia)","q":"Where are the gametangia (antheridia and archegonia) located?","ok":"On the haploid gametophyte; in seed plants, the gametophytes are tiny and stay inside the sporophyte","wrong":["On the sporophyte's sporangia","Inside the seed coat of every plant","In sori on the underside of fern leaves"],"ex":"Gametangia are part of the gametophyte: antheridia make sperm and archegonia make eggs. Sori contain sporangia, which make spores, not gametes.","img":null},"HW19":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q8 (heterosporous)","q":"What does it mean for a plant to be heterosporous?","ok":"It makes two kinds of spores: megaspores that become female gametophytes and microspores that become male gametophytes","wrong":["It makes spores by both mitosis and meiosis","It makes both spores and seeds","Its megaspores become male gametophytes"],"ex":"Heterospory means two spore types. Every seed plant is heterosporous; most seedless plants are homosporous, with one kind of spore that grows into a bisexual gametophyte. (The clicker question in Ch 29 tests the mix-up: megaspores make <i>female</i> gametophytes.)","img":null},"HW20":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q9 (seeds)","q":"How do gymnosperms and angiosperms differ in where their seeds develop?","ok":"Gymnosperm seeds are \"naked\" on the scales of cones; angiosperm seeds are enclosed in an ovary that becomes a fruit","wrong":["Gymnosperm seeds are inside fruits; angiosperm seeds are naked","Only angiosperms make seeds","Both enclose their seeds in ovaries"],"ex":"Gymno- means naked: the ovules sit exposed on sporophylls (cone scales). Angio- means vessel: the ovules are enclosed in a carpel's ovary, which matures into fruit.","img":null},"HW21":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q9 (food supply)","q":"How does the food supply of a gymnosperm seed differ from that of an angiosperm seed?","ok":"Gymnosperms: haploid female gametophyte tissue; angiosperms: triploid (3n) endosperm from double fertilization","wrong":["Gymnosperms: triploid endosperm; angiosperms: haploid gametophyte tissue","Both are fed by soil nutrients through the seed coat","Both are fed by the parent's phloem after the seed falls"],"ex":"Only angiosperms have double fertilization, which makes endosperm (3n). In a pine seed, the embryo feeds on the female gametophyte (n).","img":null},"HW22":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q9 (features)","q":"Which features are found in angiosperms but not gymnosperms?","ok":"Flowers, fruits and double fertilization","wrong":["Pollen, ovules and seeds","Heterospory and reduced gametophytes","Vascular tissue and a dominant sporophyte"],"ex":"Pollen, ovules, seeds, heterospory and reduced gametophytes are shared by all seed plants. Flowers, fruits and double fertilization belong to angiosperms alone.","img":null},"HW23":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q10","q":"What is a seed made of?","ok":"An embryo, a food supply, and a protective seed coat","wrong":["A spore, a sporangium and a cuticle","A megaspore, pollen and the ovary wall","A male gametophyte and endosperm only"],"ex":"A seed is a sporophyte embryo packaged with stored food, inside a seed coat that develops from the ovule's integuments. The clicker question on the ancient date palm seed hinges on this stored food.","img":null},"HW24":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q11 (adaptations)","q":"Besides seeds, which adaptations do all seed plants share?","ok":"Reduced gametophytes, heterospory, ovules and pollen","wrong":["Flowers, fruits and double fertilization","Free-living gametophytes and swimming sperm","Homospory and sori"],"ex":"These are the five derived traits of seed plants: seeds, reduced gametophytes, heterospory, ovules and pollen. Flowers, fruits and double fertilization are angiosperm-only.","img":null},"HW25":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q11 (why seeds win)","q":"Why are seeds an advantage over spores?","ok":"A seed holds a multicellular embryo with stored food in a protective coat, so it can stay dormant and wait for good conditions","wrong":["Seeds are single cells, so they spread more easily","Seeds photosynthesize on their own","Seeds don't need a sporophyte parent"],"ex":"A spore is a single cell. A seed already contains an embryo, food and a coat, so it can stay dormant for days to years and germinate when conditions are favorable (see the seed dormancy clicker question).","img":null},"HW26":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q11 (pine life cycle)","q":"In the life cycle of a pine (a gymnosperm), which statement is correct?","ok":"Pollen cones make microspores that become pollen; ovulate cones hold ovules, where a megaspore becomes the female gametophyte","wrong":["The pine tree is the gametophyte generation","Sperm swim through water to reach the ovulate cone","Fertilization happens the moment pollen lands on the cone"],"ex":"The tree is the sporophyte. Pollen lands on an ovule, and a pollen tube grows slowly; fertilization usually happens more than a year after pollination. The zygote becomes an embryo inside a naked seed on the cone scale.","img":null},"HW27":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q11 (angiosperm life cycle)","q":"What does the female gametophyte (embryo sac) of an angiosperm contain?","ok":"An egg, two synergids, three antipodal cells, and a central cell with two polar nuclei","wrong":["Four haploid eggs","One egg and two sperm","A pollen tube and a tube nucleus"],"ex":"One of the four megaspores survives and divides to form the embryo sac. A pollen tube enters through the micropyle and delivers two sperm; one synergid dies as the tube arrives.","img":null},"HW28":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q11 (double fertilization)","q":"In double fertilization, what does each of the two sperm do?","ok":"One fuses with the egg to make the 2n zygote; the other fuses with the two polar nuclei to make 3n endosperm","wrong":["Both fertilize the egg","One fertilizes the egg and the other fertilizes a synergid","One fertilizes the egg and the other guides the pollen tube"],"ex":"This is why angiosperm seeds contain both diploid (embryo) and triploid (endosperm) cells. Afterward the ovule becomes a seed and the ovary becomes a fruit.","img":null},"HW29":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q12 (function of flowers)","q":"What is the function of flowers?","ok":"Sexual reproduction: they make the gametophytes and, in many species, attract pollinators","wrong":["Photosynthesis","Storing food for the embryo","Absorbing water from the air"],"ex":"A flower is the angiosperm structure specialized for sexual reproduction. Animal pollinators carry pollen more directly than wind; wind-pollinated flowers tend to be small, green and inconspicuous.","img":null},"HW30":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q12 (origin of flower parts)","q":"Where do the parts of a flower come from, in evolutionary terms?","ok":"They are modified leaves: sepals, petals, stamens and carpels form four rings (whorls) on a compressed shoot","wrong":["They are modified roots","They are fused gametophytes","They are modified fruits"],"ex":"A flower is a specialized shoot with up to four whorls of modified leaves. Stamens and carpels are sporophylls, the modified leaves that bear sporangia.","img":null},"HW31":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q12 (flower parts)","q":"Which list names the parts of a carpel?","ok":"Stigma, style and ovary (with ovules inside)","wrong":["Anther and filament","Sepal and petal","Anther, style and ovule"],"ex":"The carpel is the female part: a sticky stigma catches pollen, the style leads down to the ovary, and the ovary holds the ovules. A stamen (anther and filament) is the male part, and its anther makes pollen.","img":null},"HW32":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q13 (fruit)","q":"What is a fruit?","ok":"A mature ovary (sometimes with other flower parts)","wrong":["A mature ovule","A mature anther","A fertilized egg"],"ex":"After fertilization, ovules become seeds and the ovary wall thickens into a fruit. A mature ovule is a seed, not a fruit.","img":null},"HW33":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q13 (fitness)","q":"How do fruits add to the fitness of angiosperms?","ok":"They protect the seeds and help disperse them by wind, water or animals","wrong":["They photosynthesize to feed the seed","They attract pollinators","They provide the 3n endosperm"],"ex":"Fruits carry seeds away from the parent: winged fruits ride the wind, burrs hitch rides on fur, and fleshy fruits are eaten and the seeds deposited elsewhere. Flowers attract pollinators; fruits disperse seeds.","img":null},"HW34":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q14 (monocots)","q":"Which set of traits describes a monocot?","ok":"One cotyledon, parallel leaf veins, scattered vascular bundles, fibrous roots, flower parts in threes","wrong":["Two cotyledons, net-like veins, vascular bundles in a ring, a taproot, flower parts in fours or fives","One cotyledon, net-like veins, a taproot, flower parts in fives","No cotyledons, no veins, flower parts in twos"],"ex":"Monocots include grasses, lilies, orchids and palms. Eudicots have the opposite set of traits.","img":null},"HW35":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q14 (eudicots)","q":"A plant has two cotyledons, net-like leaf veins, vascular bundles in a ring, a taproot and flowers with parts in fives. It is a","ok":"eudicot","wrong":["monocot","gymnosperm","fern"],"ex":"These are all eudicot traits; eudicot pollen also has three openings, while monocot pollen has one. Roses, beans and sunflowers are eudicots.","img":null},"HW36":{"subject":"Signals","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q15 (etiolation)","q":"What is etiolation?","ok":"Growth adapted to darkness: pale, long stems with small unexpanded leaves and short roots","wrong":["Greening of a shoot after it is exposed to light","Bending of a shoot toward light","Leaves dropping in autumn"],"ex":"A potato sprouting in a dark cellar is etiolated. It puts its energy into stem elongation to reach light, and it makes no chlorophyll because there is no light to use.","img":null},"HW37":{"subject":"Signals","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q15 (de-etiolation)","q":"What happens during de-etiolation (\"greening\")?","ok":"After light exposure, stem elongation slows, leaves expand, roots grow, and the shoot makes chlorophyll","wrong":["The shoot grows even longer and stays pale","The plant drops its leaves","The seed goes dormant"],"ex":"Light switches the shoot to normal growth. De-etiolation activates enzymes that work directly in photosynthesis, enzymes that supply chlorophyll precursors, and enzymes that change hormone levels.","img":["../drill/img/deetiolation.jpg","Signal transduction in de-etiolation: phytochrome, cGMP and Ca2+, protein kinases, transcription factors"]},"HW38":{"subject":"Signals","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q15 (three steps)","q":"What are the three steps of a signal transduction pathway, in order?","ok":"Reception, transduction, response","wrong":["Transduction, reception, response","Response, reception, transduction","Reception, response, transduction"],"ex":"Reception: a receptor protein detects the signal. Transduction: relay proteins and second messengers carry and amplify it. Response: a cellular activity changes.","img":null},"HW39":{"subject":"Signals","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q15 (reception)","q":"In de-etiolation, what is the receptor?","ok":"Phytochrome, a light-detecting protein","wrong":["cGMP","Auxin","A transcription factor"],"ex":"Receptors are proteins that change shape in response to a specific stimulus. In de-etiolation, the receptor is phytochrome in the cytoplasm.","img":["../drill/img/deetiolation.jpg","Signal transduction in de-etiolation: phytochrome, cGMP and Ca2+, protein kinases, transcription factors"]},"HW40":{"subject":"Signals","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q15 (transduction)","q":"Which second messengers carry the signal in de-etiolation?","ok":"Ca<sup>2+</sup> ions and cyclic GMP (cGMP)","wrong":["ATP and NADPH","Auxin and ethylene","DNA and RNA"],"ex":"Activated phytochrome opens Ca<sup>2+</sup> channels, raising Ca<sup>2+</sup> in the cytosol, and activates an enzyme that makes cGMP. Both second messengers activate protein kinases.","img":["../drill/img/deetiolation.jpg","Signal transduction in de-etiolation: phytochrome, cGMP and Ca2+, protein kinases, transcription factors"]},"HW41":{"subject":"Signals","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q15 (response)","q":"In the response step, how does a signal pathway change a cell's activity?","ok":"By post-translational modification of existing proteins (phosphorylation by kinases) or by transcriptional regulation (transcription factors turn genes on or off)","wrong":["By changing the DNA sequence of the cell","By destroying the receptor","Only by opening stomata"],"ex":"Protein kinases phosphorylate proteins to activate them, and phosphatases switch the pathway off. Transcription factors can be activators (more transcription) or repressors (less).","img":["../drill/img/deetiolation.jpg","Signal transduction in de-etiolation: phytochrome, cGMP and Ca2+, protein kinases, transcription factors"]},"HW42":{"subject":"Signals","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q16 (bending)","q":"What causes a coleoptile to bend toward light?","ok":"Auxin moves to the shaded side, so cells there elongate faster than cells on the lit side","wrong":["Cells on the lit side elongate faster","Light kills cells on the shaded side","Cytokinin builds up on the lit side"],"ex":"Phototropism comes from unequal growth: more auxin on the shaded side means more elongation there, which tips the shoot toward the light.","img":null},"HW43":{"subject":"Signals","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q16 (where the signal is made)","q":"Where is the phototropism signal produced?","ok":"In the tip of the coleoptile","wrong":["In the roots","In the seed","Along the whole stem equally"],"ex":"The Darwins found that a coleoptile bends only when its tip is lit: removing the tip or covering it with an opaque cap stops the bending, while shielding the bending region doesn't.","img":null},"HW44":{"subject":"Signals","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q16 (which part responds)","q":"Which part of the coleoptile bends, and how does the signal get there?","ok":"The region below the tip; a mobile chemical moves down from the tip","wrong":["The tip itself; nerve-like electrical signals","The roots; signals through the xylem","The whole seedling at once; by light alone"],"ex":"Boysen-Jensen showed that the tip's signal passes through permeable gelatin but not impermeable mica, so it is a chemical. Went then collected it in agar blocks: auxin (IAA).","img":null},"HW45":{"subject":"Signals","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q16 (acid growth hypothesis)","q":"Plant cells are surrounded by rigid walls. According to the acid growth hypothesis, how does auxin let them elongate?","ok":"Auxin activates proton pumps; H<sup>+</sup> pumped into the wall lowers its pH, activating expansins that loosen it, so water uptake can stretch the cell","wrong":["Auxin dissolves the cell wall completely","Auxin pumps H<sup>+</sup> into the cytoplasm, making the wall stiffer","Auxin triggers cell division instead of elongation"],"ex":"Low pH activates expansins and wall-loosening enzymes that cleave cross-linking polysaccharides. Cellulose microfibrils then slide apart, the vacuole takes up water, and turgor stretches the cell.","img":["../drill/img/acid-growth.jpg","The acid growth hypothesis: proton pumps acidify the cell wall, expansins loosen it, and the cell elongates"]},"HW46":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q17","q":"Which is an example of a plant hormone whose effect depends on its concentration?","ok":"Auxin: low concentrations stimulate stem cell elongation, while high concentrations inhibit it","wrong":["Abscisic acid: low levels close stomata and high levels open them","Gibberellin: low levels cause leaf fall and high levels cause dwarfism","Ethylene: it works only at one fixed concentration"],"ex":"Plant responses depend on how much of a hormone is present. Auxin is the classic example: very high levels inhibit elongation, partly by triggering ethylene production. The ethylene triple response also gets stronger as ethylene concentration rises.","img":null},"HW47":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q18 (main role)","q":"What do cytokinins do?","ok":"Stimulate cell division (cytokinesis), working with auxin to control cell division and differentiation","wrong":["Close stomata during drought","Ripen fruit","Keep seeds dormant"],"ex":"Cytokinins are made in actively growing tissues (roots, embryos and fruits). The ratio of cytokinin to auxin steers what a group of cells becomes.","img":null},"HW48":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q18 (apical dominance)","q":"How do cytokinins affect apical dominance?","ok":"They promote growth of axillary (lateral) buds, opposing the suppression from the terminal bud","wrong":["They strengthen the terminal bud's suppression of axillary buds","They have no role in branching","They cause the terminal bud to fall off"],"ex":"Cytokinins, auxin and strigolactone interact to control apical dominance. Remove the terminal bud (the source of auxin) and the axillary buds grow, making the plant bushier.","img":null},"HW49":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q18 (anti-aging)","q":"How do cytokinins slow the aging of some plant organs?","ok":"By inhibiting protein breakdown, stimulating RNA and protein synthesis, and drawing nutrients in from nearby tissues","wrong":["By triggering a burst of ethylene","By closing the stomata","By breaking down chlorophyll"],"ex":"That's why florists use cytokinin sprays to keep cut flowers fresh. Ethylene does the opposite: it promotes senescence.","img":null},"HW50":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q19","q":"What is the role of gibberellins?","ok":"Stem elongation, fruit growth and seed germination","wrong":["Closing stomata and keeping seeds dormant","Leaf abscission and fruit ripening","Defense against insect herbivores"],"ex":"Gibberellins stimulate growth of stems and leaves, help seeds break dormancy and use stored reserves, and with auxin let fruit set. Growers spray them on Thompson seedless grapes to get larger grapes.","img":null},"HW51":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q20","q":"Which molecule is responsible for fruit ripening and the abscission (falling) of leaves?","ok":"Ethylene","wrong":["Cytokinin","Gibberellin","Brassinosteroids"],"ex":"A burst of ethylene triggers ripening. A shift in the balance of auxin and ethylene controls leaf abscission in autumn. Ethylene also drives the triple response and senescence.","img":null},"HW52":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q21","q":"Brassinosteroids are similar to which hormones?","ok":"The steroid sex hormones of animals (chemically similar to cholesterol)","wrong":["Animal insulin","Ethylene gas","Abscisic acid"],"ex":"Brassinosteroids are steroids, chemically similar to cholesterol and to animal sex hormones. In plants their effects resemble auxin's: they induce cell elongation and division.","img":null},"HW53":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q22 (roles)","q":"What are the major roles of abscisic acid (ABA)?","ok":"Slowing growth, keeping seeds dormant, and helping plants tolerate drought by closing stomata","wrong":["Ripening fruit and dropping leaves","Speeding up seed germination","Causing stems to grow taller"],"ex":"ABA is the main internal signal for drought: when it accumulates, stomata close rapidly. It also keeps seeds dormant until conditions are good.","img":null},"HW54":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q22 (breaking dormancy)","q":"How is ABA-based seed dormancy broken in some seeds?","ok":"ABA is removed by heavy rain, light, or prolonged cold","wrong":["ABA levels rise sharply","The seed is buried deeper","Ethylene is washed out of the seed"],"ex":"Removing ABA lets the seed germinate when conditions are near optimal. Seeds with low or inactive ABA germinate too early (precocious germination), as in red mangroves and some maize mutants.","img":null},"HW55":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q23 (thigmomorphogenesis)","q":"How can mechanical stress change a plant's form?","ok":"Thigmomorphogenesis: plants that are rubbed or shaken grow shorter and stockier","wrong":["They grow taller and thinner","They switch to making only roots","They stop photosynthesizing"],"ex":"Rubbing the stems of young plants a couple of times a day makes them shorter than controls. Ethylene, produced under mechanical stress, is part of the cause.","img":null},"HW56":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q23 (triple response)","q":"A seedling growing upward hits a stone. Ethylene triggers the triple response. What are its three parts?","ok":"Slower stem elongation, a thicker stem, and horizontal growth","wrong":["Faster elongation, a thinner stem, and downward growth","Leaf fall, fruit ripening, and flowering","Stomatal closure, dormancy, and wilting"],"ex":"The triple response lets a shoot grow around an obstacle. Ethylene-insensitive (<i>ein</i>) mutants don't show it.","img":null},"HW57":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q23 (touch responses)","q":"Which is an example of a plant responding to touch?","ok":"Tendrils curling around a support (thigmotropism), or <i>Mimosa</i> leaflets folding when touched","wrong":["Shoots growing upward against gravity","Seeds germinating after red light","Leaves dropping in autumn"],"ex":"Thigmotropism is directional growth in response to touch, common in vines. <i>Mimosa pudica</i> folds rapidly through turgor changes triggered by electrical impulses (action potentials).","img":null},"HW58":{"subject":"Light & time","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q24 (Pr and Pfr)","q":"Phytochrome exists in two forms. How does light switch between them?","ok":"Red light converts Pr to Pfr (the active form); far-red light converts Pfr back to Pr","wrong":["Far-red light converts Pr to Pfr; red light converts Pfr to Pr","Blue light converts Pr to Pfr; UV converts it back","Darkness converts Pr to Pfr instantly"],"ex":"Pfr triggers seed germination, inhibits vertical growth and stimulates branching, sets internal clocks, and controls flowering. In the dark, Pfr slowly reverts to Pr in some species.","img":null},"HW59":{"subject":"Light & time","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q24 (final flash)","q":"Lettuce seeds get flashes of red, then far-red, then red light. What happens?","ok":"They germinate, because the last flash was red","wrong":["They don't germinate, because far-red light was included","The result can't be predicted","They germinate only if the first flash was red"],"ex":"Red and far-red effects are reversible, so the last exposure decides. It is red here, leaving phytochrome mostly as Pfr, which promotes germination.","img":null},"HW60":{"subject":"Light & time","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q24 (sunlight)","q":"Why does sunlight trigger germination even though it contains both red and far-red light?","ok":"Pr turns into Pfr faster than Pfr turns back, so sunlight raises the Pfr:Pr ratio","wrong":["Sunlight contains no far-red light","Sunlight destroys Pr","Phytochrome ignores sunlight"],"ex":"Because conversion to Pfr is faster, full sun builds up Pfr. Under a canopy, leaves filter out red light, so far-red dominates and more phytochrome is Pr.","img":null},"HW61":{"subject":"Light & time","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q25 (short-day)","q":"When do short-day plants flower?","ok":"When the night is longer than a critical night length (usually late summer, fall or winter)","wrong":["When the night is shorter than a critical night length","Only after a flash of red light at night","At any time of year, regardless of night length"],"ex":"Short-day plants are really long-night plants. The critical night length sets the minimum number of hours of darkness they need to flower.","img":null},"HW62":{"subject":"Light & time","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q25 (long-day)","q":"When do long-day plants flower?","ok":"When the night is shorter than a critical night length (usually late spring or early summer)","wrong":["When the night is longer than a critical night length","Only after a cold treatment","Only in complete darkness"],"ex":"For long-day (short-night) plants, the critical night length sets the maximum number of hours of darkness. A flash of red light that breaks up a long night can make them flower.","img":null},"HW63":{"subject":"Light & time","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q26","q":"Cyclic changes in plants over a roughly 24-hour period, such as flowers opening and closing, are called","ok":"circadian rhythms","wrong":["photoperiodism","phototropism","thigmomorphogenesis"],"ex":"Circadian means about a day. Circadian rhythms continue without outside cues, last about 24 hours, can be reset (entrained), and compensate for temperature. Photoperiodism is the response to day and night length across the seasons.","img":null},"HW64":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q27 (stress)","q":"What does \"stress\" mean for a plant?","ok":"Any environmental condition that can harm its growth, development or productivity","wrong":["Only attack by herbivores","Only lack of sunlight","The normal daily circadian rhythm"],"ex":"Stress can be abiotic (drought, flooding, salt, heat, cold) or biotic (pathogens and herbivores). Plants cope through physiological, chemical and developmental responses.","img":null},"HW65":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q27 (drought)","q":"How does a plant cope with drought?","ok":"It makes ABA, which closes stomata to reduce water loss","wrong":["It forms air tubes in its roots","It makes heat-shock proteins","It makes antifreeze proteins"],"ex":"ABA is the main drought signal. Closing stomata cuts transpiration; leaves may also roll or wilt to reduce their exposed surface.","img":null},"HW66":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q27 (flooding)","q":"How does a plant cope with flooding?","ok":"It forms air tubes in the roots that help them survive low oxygen","wrong":["It closes stomata with ABA","It makes antifreeze proteins","It stores salt in its leaves"],"ex":"Waterlogged soil has little oxygen. Ethylene triggers controlled cell death in the root cortex, which opens air channels (aerenchyma) that carry oxygen to submerged roots. That's why \"flooding → ABA in the shoots\" is the wrong pairing in a Ch 39 clicker question.","img":null},"HW67":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q27 (salt)","q":"How does a plant cope with salty soil?","ok":"It makes solutes it can tolerate at high concentrations, so it doesn't lose water by osmosis","wrong":["It forms air tubes in its roots","It makes heat-shock proteins","It opens its stomata wider"],"ex":"Salty soil pulls water out of roots by osmosis. Producing compatible solutes raises the cells' own solute concentration, so water stays in.","img":null},"HW68":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q27 (heat)","q":"How does a plant cope with excessive heat?","ok":"It makes heat-shock proteins that help keep other proteins from denaturing","wrong":["It makes antifreeze proteins","It forms air tubes in its roots","It drops all its roots"],"ex":"Heat-shock proteins act as chaperones that help other proteins hold their shape at high temperatures.","img":null},"HW69":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q27 (cold)","q":"How does a plant cope with cold?","ok":"It adjusts membrane fluidity, avoids osmotic water loss, and makes antifreeze proteins against ice crystals","wrong":["It makes heat-shock proteins","It forms air tubes in the roots","It closes its flowers"],"ex":"Cold stiffens membranes, so plants change their lipid makeup to keep them fluid. Antifreeze proteins block ice crystals from forming and damaging cells.","img":null},"HW70":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q28 (defenses)","q":"Which list names plant defenses against pathogens?","ok":"The epidermis and periderm as a barrier, the hypersensitive response, and systemic acquired resistance","wrong":["Antibodies and phagocytic white blood cells","Fever and inflammation","Making more gibberellins to outgrow the pathogen"],"ex":"The first line of defense is the physical barrier of the epidermis and periderm. Pathogens that get in through wounds or stomata trigger chemical defenses. Plants don't make antibodies, which belong to the vertebrate adaptive immune system.","img":null},"HW71":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q28 (hypersensitive response)","q":"What does the hypersensitive response do?","ok":"It kills cells near the infection site, makes enzymes that attack the pathogen, and changes cell walls to confine it","wrong":["It spreads the pathogen to the rest of the plant","It makes antibodies against the pathogen","It makes the plant grow faster"],"ex":"By killing its own cells around the infection, the plant walls off the pathogen and leaves visible lesions (see the infected tobacco leaf on the slides).","img":null},"HW72":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q28 (salicylic acid)","q":"What is the role of salicylic acid in plant defense?","ok":"It triggers systemic acquired resistance, so the whole plant can respond quickly to a later infection","wrong":["It kills the pathogen directly at the wound","It closes the stomata during drought","It ripens fruit"],"ex":"Methylsalicylic acid made around the infection site travels through the phloem. Healthy distant cells convert it to salicylic acid, which switches on defense genes (PR proteins).","img":null},"HW73":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q28 (herbivores)","q":"How can a maize plant defend itself against caterpillars that are eating it?","ok":"Chemicals in the caterpillar's saliva trigger it to release volatile compounds that attract parasitoid wasps, which lay their eggs in the caterpillars","wrong":["It makes antibodies against the caterpillar","It closes its stomata","It drops its leaves"],"ex":"Plants also use thorns and distasteful or toxic secondary compounds, and some \"recruit\" predators, like the wasps here, that attack the herbivore.","img":null},"HW74":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q36 (what lichens are)","q":"What are lichens?","ok":"A symbiotic association between a fungus and a photosynthetic microbe (a green alga or a cyanobacterium)","wrong":["A kind of moss","A single fungal species","A plant growing on a fungus as a parasite"],"ex":"A lichen looks like one organism but is two (or more) partners, often among the first life on bare rock.","img":null},"HW75":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q36 (the fungus's role)","q":"What does the fungus contribute to a lichen?","ok":"The body and shape: it absorbs water and minerals, protects the partner, and secretes acids that break down rock","wrong":["Sugars from photosynthesis","Fixed nitrogen in every lichen","Chlorophyll"],"ex":"The photosynthetic partner provides carbon compounds (sugars), and cyanobacteria also fix nitrogen. The fungus provides the structure, water and minerals.","img":null},"HW76":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q37","q":"Why are fungi important in the biosphere?","ok":"They decompose dead matter and recycle nutrients, form mutualisms (mycorrhizae, lichens), and some are pathogens","wrong":["They are the main producers in most ecosystems","They fix most of the nitrogen on Earth","They are the main food of large animals"],"ex":"Fungi break down even cellulose and lignin and return carbon, nitrogen and minerals to the ecosystem. Most land plants depend on mycorrhizal fungi. Fungi also give us food, antibiotics (penicillin) and model organisms (yeast).","img":null},"HW77":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q38","q":"What are fruiting bodies, such as mushrooms?","ok":"Reproductive structures made of tightly packed hyphae that produce and release spores","wrong":["The fungus's feeding organs","Single giant cells","Fruits that the fungus steals from plants"],"ex":"Most of a fungus is the hidden mycelium. The fruiting body is the short-lived, spore-making part above ground: basidiocarps (mushrooms) in basidiomycetes, ascocarps in ascomycetes.","img":null},"HW78":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q39 (types)","q":"What types of reproduction occur in fungi?","ok":"Both sexual and asexual; both usually produce spores","wrong":["Only sexual reproduction","Only asexual reproduction","Only binary fission"],"ex":"Asexual spores form by mitosis (e.g., conidia, or budding in yeasts). Sexual spores form after the fusion of mating partners and meiosis.","img":null},"HW79":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q39 (sexual sequence)","q":"What is the order of events in fungal sexual reproduction?","ok":"Plasmogamy (cytoplasm fuses) → heterokaryotic stage → karyogamy (nuclei fuse) → meiosis → haploid spores","wrong":["Karyogamy → plasmogamy → meiosis → spores","Meiosis → plasmogamy → karyogamy → spores","Mitosis → karyogamy → plasmogamy → spores"],"ex":"In fungi, the cytoplasm and the nuclei fuse at different times, sometimes years apart. Meiosis follows the fusion of nuclei, which is the answer to the Ch 31 clicker question.","img":null},"HW80":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q40","q":"What are hyphae and the mycelium?","ok":"Hyphae are tiny tube-like filaments; the mycelium is the interwoven mass of hyphae that forms the fungus's body","wrong":["Hyphae are spores; the mycelium is the fruiting body","Hyphae are roots; the mycelium is the stem","Hyphae are nuclei; the mycelium is the cell wall"],"ex":"Hyphae have chitin walls. They are either septate (divided by cross-walls with pores) or coenocytic (no cross-walls, many nuclei). The mycelium's huge surface area makes it efficient at absorbing food.","img":null},"HW81":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q41","q":"How do fungi get their nutrients?","ok":"They are heterotrophs that absorb food: they secrete enzymes into their surroundings to break down molecules, then absorb the small products","wrong":["By photosynthesis","By swallowing food and digesting it inside the body","By chemosynthesis from rocks"],"ex":"Fungi digest food outside their bodies with exoenzymes, then absorb it. They can be decomposers, parasites (some use haustoria to take nutrients from hosts) or mutualists.","img":null},"HW82":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q42 (zygomycetes)","q":"What happens in the life cycle of a zygomycete such as black bread mold?","ok":"Hyphae of opposite mating types fuse to form a tough zygosporangium, which undergoes karyogamy and meiosis and releases spores when conditions improve","wrong":["Eight ascospores form in a sac","Basidiospores form on gills","Flagellated spores swim in water"],"ex":"The zygosporangium is resistant to freezing and drying. Zygomycetes also reproduce asexually from sporangia on upright hyphae, which is the black fuzz on moldy bread.","img":null},"HW83":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q42 (ascomycetes)","q":"What happens in the life cycle of an ascomycete?","ok":"After karyogamy and meiosis, spores form inside a sac (ascus), usually eight ascospores in a row, within an ascocarp; asexually they make conidia","wrong":["A dikaryotic mushroom forms basidia on its gills","Mating types fuse into a zygosporangium","Flagellated zoospores swim to new hosts"],"ex":"Asci are packed into a fruiting body called an ascocarp. Eight ascospores in a row means an ascomycete (a Ch 31 clicker question).","img":null},"HW84":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q42 (basidiomycetes)","q":"What happens in the life cycle of a basidiomycete (a mushroom)?","ok":"A long-lived dikaryotic mycelium forms a basidiocarp; karyogamy and meiosis in club-shaped basidia on the gills make basidiospores","wrong":["Eight spores form in a sac","Mating types fuse into a zygosporangium","Spores have flagella"],"ex":"Most of the life cycle is dikaryotic: each cell has two different haploid nuclei. The mushroom you eat is made of dikaryotic cells (a Ch 31 clicker question).","img":null},"HW85":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q43","q":"What is special about chytrids?","ok":"They have flagellated spores (zoospores), the only fungi that do, which suggests they resemble the earliest fungi","wrong":["They have no cell walls","They form most mushrooms","They form mycorrhizae with most plants"],"ex":"Chytrids live in lakes and soil and have chitin walls. One chytrid is killing amphibians worldwide.","img":null},"HW86":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q44 (zygomycetes)","q":"Which feature is special to zygomycetes?","ok":"Resistant zygosporangia; they are fast-growing molds such as black bread mold (<i>Rhizopus</i>)","wrong":["Asci with eight spores","Club-shaped basidia","Flagellated spores"],"ex":"Zygomycetes have mostly coenocytic hyphae. They are the fuzzy black mold you throw away before your parents visit.","img":null},"HW87":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q44 (ascomycetes)","q":"Which feature is special to ascomycetes (sac fungi)?","ok":"Spores made in sacs (asci), often within an ascocarp; they include yeasts, morels, truffles and <i>Penicillium</i>, and are the fungal partner in most lichens","wrong":["Club-shaped basidia on gills","Resistant zygosporangia","Flagellated zoospores"],"ex":"Ascomycetes range from single-celled yeasts to cup fungi. Asexually they make conidia at the tips of hyphae.","img":null},"HW88":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q44 (basidiomycetes)","q":"Which feature is special to basidiomycetes (club fungi)?","ok":"Club-shaped basidia and a long-lived dikaryotic mycelium; they include mushrooms, shelf fungi, rusts and smuts, and are key decomposers of wood","wrong":["Spores in sacs (asci)","Resistant zygosporangia","Flagellated zoospores"],"ex":"Basidiomycetes are good at breaking down lignin. A fairy ring is the edge of one basidiomycete mycelium growing outward.","img":null},"C290":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 29","tag":"Charophytes","q":"Which of the following are the closest algal relatives of land plants?","ok":"charophytes","wrong":["psilophytes","chrysophytes","bacillariophytes","rhodophytes"],"ex":"Charophytes are green algae and the closest living relatives of land plants.","img":null},"C291":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 29","tag":"Life cycle","q":"The relationship between a gametophyte and a sporophyte in a liverwort is like the relationship between","ok":"a parent and a child.","wrong":["a brother and a sister.","a grandparent and a grandchild.","an uncle and a nephew.","two cousins."],"ex":"The sporophyte grows from the zygote made by the gametophyte's gametes, and stays attached to it: the gametophyte is the parent.","img":null},"C292":{"subject":"Seedless vascular","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 29","tag":"Vascular tissue","q":"Plants that evolved vascular tissue are more advanced than plants without vascular tissue. One of the consequences is that vascular tissue enabled plants to","ok":"grow taller.","wrong":["reproduce via spores.","store water.","develop stomata.","support large gametophytes."],"ex":"Lignified xylem holds the plant up and moves water up its height. Bryophytes also reproduce by spores and many have stomata.","img":null},"C293":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 29","tag":"Life cycle figure","q":"One thing you should be able to conclude from this figure is that","ok":"gametophytes have fewer chromosomes than sporophytes do.","wrong":["gametophytes evolved before sporophytes.","gametophytes grow from sporophytes.","gametophyte cells come about by mitosis; sporophyte cells come about by meiosis."],"ex":"Gametophytes are haploid (n), so they have half as many chromosomes as diploid (2n) sporophytes.","img":["../drill/img/moss-cycle.jpg","The moss life cycle: haploid gametophytes (n) and the diploid sporophyte (2n) growing from them"]},"C294":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 29","tag":"Stomata","q":"Stomata are found in every group of sporophyte plants except the liverworts. According to the hypothesis that stomata evolved only once among the bryophytes, this is evidence that","ok":"liverworts resemble the most primitive plants.","wrong":["liverworts don't need to exchange gases with the atmosphere.","liverworts have lost the ability to make stomata.","liverworts are able to fix nitrogen.","gametophytes are more important in liverworts."],"ex":"If stomata evolved once and liverworts never had them, liverworts must have branched off before stomata appeared: the earliest-diverging lineage.","img":null},"C295":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 29","tag":"Mosses","q":"Which of the following plants have a sporophyte that is nutritionally dependent on the photosynthetic gametophyte?","ok":"mosses","wrong":["ferns","whisk ferns","horsetails"],"ex":"A brown moss sporophyte grows out of the top of the green gametophyte and depends on it. Ferns, whisk ferns and horsetails are vascular plants with independent sporophytes.","img":["../drill/img/moss-cycle.jpg","The moss life cycle: haploid gametophytes (n) and the diploid sporophyte (2n) growing from them"]},"C296":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 29","tag":"Seed plants","q":"Which evolutionary innovation of seed plants enabled them to outcompete ferns and other seedless plants that dominated through the end of the Carboniferous period?","ok":"reduced, dependent gametophytes","wrong":["heterospory","vascular systems","flowers"],"ex":"Gametophytes protected inside the sporophyte don't dry out, a big advantage as the climate dried after the Carboniferous. Heterospory and vascular tissue already existed in seedless plants; flowers came much later.","img":null},"C297":{"subject":"Seedless vascular","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 29","tag":"First forests","q":"Seedless vascular plants affected Earth and its other life in all of the following ways except","ok":"plants reduce the rate at which chemicals such as calcium and magnesium are released from rocks into soil.","wrong":["early forests contributed to a large drop in CO2 levels.","CO2 removed from the air was stored in marine rocks.","seedless vascular plants forming the first forests became coal."],"ex":"Plants <i>increase</i> the release of minerals from rocks (chemical weathering), as the moss experiment shows. That weathering helped pull CO<sub>2</sub> out of the air.","img":null},"C298":{"subject":"Seedless vascular","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 29","tag":"Nonvascular","q":"Which of the following are not considered a nonvascular plant?","ok":"lycophytes","wrong":["liverworts","mosses","hornworts","bryophytes"],"ex":"Lycophytes (club mosses) have vascular tissue despite their name. Liverworts, mosses and hornworts are bryophytes, the nonvascular plants.","img":null},"C299":{"subject":"Seedless vascular","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 29","tag":"Terms","q":"Which term is not correctly matched with its definition?","ok":"Megaspores: develop into male gametophytes","wrong":["Sporophylls: modified leaves that bear sporangia","Strobili: cone-like structures formed from groups of sporophylls in lycophytes and gymnosperms","Sori: clusters of sporangia produced by fern sporophylls"],"ex":"Megaspores develop into <i>female</i> gametophytes; microspores develop into male gametophytes.","img":null},"C2910":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 29","tag":"Experiment: setup","q":"Moss experiment: researchers put granite or andesite rock in microcosms with water plus macerated moss (experimental) or filtered water without the moss (control), and measured minerals after 130 days. How did the setup of the control and experimental microcosms differ?","ok":"Control microcosms did not contain living moss, while experimental microcosms did contain living moss.","wrong":["Control microcosms contained minerals in the water, while experimental microcosms did not.","Control microcosms did not contain rock of volcanic origin, while experimental microcosms did.","Control microcosms contained water, while experimental microcosms did not."],"ex":"The only difference was the living moss: both kinds of microcosm had the same rocks and water.","img":null},"C2911":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 29","tag":"Experiment: control","q":"Why did the researchers add filtrate from which macerated moss had been removed to the control microcosms?","ok":"to control for the possibility that the filtrate contained material derived from moss","wrong":["to control for the possibility that the filtrate included nutrients released from granite or andesite","to provide replication","to control for the possibility that the filtrate contained living moss cells"],"ex":"Chopped moss releases chemicals into the water. Giving the controls the same water without moss cells means any difference comes from the living moss itself.","img":null},"C2912":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 29","tag":"Experiment: results","q":"The bar graphs show the mean total amount of each element weathered from rocks. Overall, what is the effect of moss on the chemical weathering of rock?","ok":"Moss increases the chemical weathering of rock.","wrong":["Moss does not increase the chemical weathering of rock.","It is not clear whether or not moss increases the chemical weathering of rock."],"ex":"For every element and both rocks, the experimental (moss) bars are taller than the control bars.","img":["../drill/img/weathering.jpg","Bar graphs: calcium, magnesium and potassium weathered from granite (left) and andesite (right), control vs. experimental (moss) microcosms"]},"C2913":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 29","tag":"Experiment: abiotic","q":"Why were mineral nutrients released in the control microcosms?","ok":"The release of mineral nutrients in the control microcosms indicates the extent of abiotic weathering of rock.","wrong":["It indicates the extent of biotic weathering of rock.","It was due to organic acids released by mosses.","There was no release of mineral nutrients in the control microcosms."],"ex":"Without living moss, rock still weathers slowly through non-living (abiotic) processes. That's the baseline the moss effect is measured against.","img":["../drill/img/weathering.jpg","Bar graphs: calcium, magnesium and potassium weathered from granite (left) and andesite (right), control vs. experimental (moss) microcosms"]},"C2914":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 29","tag":"Experiment: granite vs. andesite","q":"Review the bar graph. How does the moss's chemical weathering effect on granite compare to its effect on andesite?","ok":"Moss increased the release of all three elements (calcium, magnesium, and potassium) from both granite and andesite.","wrong":["The results are not conclusive about the effect of moss on granite and andesite.","Moss did not alter the release of calcium, magnesium, and potassium from granite and andesite.","Moss decreased the release of all three elements from both granite and andesite."],"ex":"Every experimental bar is higher than its control, for both rock types.","img":["../drill/img/weathering.jpg","Bar graphs: calcium, magnesium and potassium weathered from granite (left) and andesite (right), control vs. experimental (moss) microcosms"]},"C2915":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 29","tag":"Experiment: assumption","q":"The researchers added weathering by nonvascular plants to models of the Ordovician climate, which then predicted lower CO2 and global cooling. Which assumption did they make?","ok":"They assumed that the effect of Ordovician mosses on the chemical weathering of rock was similar to the weathering caused by the modern moss P. patens.","wrong":["They assumed the modern moss P. patens increased the availability of mineral nutrients by breaking down granite and andesite.","They assumed that chemical weathering by mosses reduced atmospheric CO2 levels, producing dramatic cooling.","They assumed the modern moss P. patens increased the weathering of rock."],"ex":"They measured a modern moss but applied the result to ancient ones. The other choices are results or predictions, not assumptions.","img":null},"C2916":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 29","tag":"Experiment: conclusion","q":"How do these experimental results and models demonstrate that life has profoundly changed Earth?","ok":"The results show that early nonvascular plants could have caused enough chemical weathering of rock to reduce atmospheric CO2 levels.","wrong":["The results show that early nonvascular plants could have caused enough chemical weathering of rock to increase atmospheric CO2 levels.","The results show that nonvascular and vascular plants utilize rock-based minerals.","The results show that mineral nutrients are released by weathering in the absence of living cells."],"ex":"Chemical weathering draws CO<sub>2</sub> out of the atmosphere. More weathering means less CO<sub>2</sub>, cooling Earth enough for glaciers in the late Ordovician.","img":null},"C300":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 30","tag":"Double fertilization","q":"The reason that two sperm nuclei travel down the pollen tube is that","ok":"one fertilizes the egg, and the other combines with the two polar nuclei.","wrong":["they both stimulate growth of the pollen tube.","one fertilizes the egg, and the other fertilizes the synergid.","one is for fertilizing the egg, and one directs the pollen tube toward the micropyle.","if one fails in fertilization, there is a backup nucleus."],"ex":"This is double fertilization: egg + sperm makes the zygote (2n); polar nuclei + sperm makes the endosperm (3n).","img":null},"C301":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 30","tag":"Order of adaptations","q":"Adaptations that have contributed to the success of plants on land include seeds, vascular tissue, cuticle, and flowers. Which lists them in the order they appeared in the fossil record?","ok":"cuticle, vascular tissue, seeds, flowers","wrong":["seeds, vascular tissue, flowers, cuticle","cuticle, seeds, flowers, vascular tissue","vascular tissue, cuticle, seeds, flowers"],"ex":"Bryophytes have a cuticle, ferns add vascular tissue, gymnosperms add seeds, and angiosperms add flowers. The order matches the branching of the plant tree.","img":null},"C302":{"subject":"Seedless vascular","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 30","tag":"Key traits table","q":"In a table of key traits, bryophytes have only a cuticle. Which group has a cuticle and vascular tissue, but no seeds or flowers?","ok":"ferns (and relatives)","wrong":["bryophytes (mosses)","flowering plants","gymnosperms"],"ex":"Ferns, lycophytes and their relatives are the seedless vascular plants.","img":null},"C303":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 30","tag":"Key traits table","q":"Continuing the table: which group has a cuticle, vascular tissue and seeds, but no flowers?","ok":"gymnosperms","wrong":["ferns (and relatives)","bryophytes (mosses)","flowering plants"],"ex":"Gymnosperms (conifers, cycads, ginkgo, gnetophytes) have naked seeds and no flowers. Flowering plants have all four traits.","img":null},"C304":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 30","tag":"Double fertilization","q":"All of the following statements regarding double fertilization in the angiosperms are true except which one?","ok":"The megasporangium gives rise to four haploid eggs through meiosis.","wrong":["The angiosperm embryo is diploid.","The pollen tube reaching the micropyle results in the death of one of the synergids.","The fertilized angiosperm ovule consists of diploid and triploid cells.","One of the pollen nuclei contributes to the genome of the endosperm."],"ex":"Meiosis in the megasporangium makes four megaspores, not eggs, and three of them usually die. The survivor grows into an embryo sac with a single egg.","img":null},"C305":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 30","tag":"Wind pollination","q":"The flowers of wind-pollinated plants","ok":"are usually green and inconspicuous.","wrong":["usually have flat blades to direct the wind.","produce sticky pollen.","are usually white and small.","usually occur on separate male and female plants."],"ex":"Wind doesn't need to be attracted, so wind-pollinated flowers (grasses, many trees) skip showy petals and make lots of light, dry pollen. Sticky pollen goes with animal pollinators.","img":null},"C306":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 30","tag":"Seed food","q":"An ancient date palm seed germinated and grew into a healthy plant. What was the source of nutrients for the young sporophyte embryo during germination?","ok":"food stored within the seed","wrong":["sunlight","water taken up by the germinating seed","the surrounding soil"],"ex":"A seed packs a food supply (endosperm or cotyledons) with the embryo. The seedling lives on it until its own leaves and roots can take over.","img":null},"C307":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 30","tag":"Seed dormancy","q":"Why is seed dormancy adaptive for many plant species?","ok":"Seed dormancy allows seeds to germinate when conditions are favorable for seedling growth.","wrong":["Seed dormancy increases the longevity of an individual sporophyte plant.","Seed dormancy ensures that a seedling does not compete with its parents.","Seed dormancy ensures that a seedling does not germinate when a source of food is not available."],"ex":"A dormant seed can wait out cold, drought or shade. ABA maintains dormancy until rain, light or cold removes it.","img":null},"C310":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 31","tag":"Biomass","q":"Which individual organism in this picture probably has more biomass?","ok":"fungus","wrong":["tree"],"ex":"The honey mushroom (<i>Armillaria ostoyae</i>) in Oregon has a mycelium spreading across about 965 hectares, one of the largest organisms on Earth. The mushrooms are only its small fruiting bodies.","img":["../drill/img/armillaria.jpg","Honey mushrooms (Armillaria) growing at the base of a tree"]},"C311":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 31","tag":"Enzymes","q":"Like animals, fungi are heterotrophs and use enzymes to break down food macromolecules. What is true about these fungal enzymes that is not generally true of animal enzymes?","ok":"All of the above","wrong":["Many of them catalyze hydrolysis reactions.","They reduce the activation energy and speed up reactions.","They are proteins that are specific for digesting certain types of molecules."],"ex":"The professor's key is \"All of the above\": each statement is true of fungal enzymes. The real difference from animals is where they work: fungi secrete their enzymes outside their bodies and then absorb the products.","img":null},"C312":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 31","tag":"Roles","q":"Which of the following is a role NOT performed by any fungi?","ok":"producer","wrong":["decomposer","parasite","predator"],"ex":"Fungi have no chloroplasts, so none are producers. Some fungi are even predators that trap tiny worms with hyphae.","img":null},"C313":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 31","tag":"Chitin","q":"You are presented with several single-celled organisms, including one thought to belong to the kingdom Fungi. What unique feature helps you identify the fungus?","ok":"presence of chitinous cell walls","wrong":["presence of mitochondria","absence of chloroplasts","presence of chitin","presence of nuclei"],"ex":"Chitin by itself also occurs in arthropod exoskeletons, so it isn't unique to fungi. Cell walls made of chitin are the fungal mark.","img":null},"C314":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 31","tag":"Meiosis","q":"When does meiosis occur in fungi?","ok":"following the fusion of nuclei","wrong":["during asexual reproduction","immediately after the fusion of two fungal cells","after the fusion of cytoplasm but before the fusion of nuclei"],"ex":"Plasmogamy (fused cytoplasm) → heterokaryotic stage → karyogamy (fused nuclei, a 2n zygote) → meiosis → haploid spores.","img":null},"C315":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 31","tag":"Mating","q":"In animals, the diploid stage signals to attract a mate and checks whether the mate is compatible. Which life stage typically does this in fungi?","ok":"haploid hyphae","wrong":["haploid spores","heterokaryon hyphae","diploid hyphae","diploid spores"],"ex":"In fungi, haploid hyphae release pheromones and fuse with compatible mates. The diploid stage is brief, often only the zygote.","img":null},"C316":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 31","tag":"Bread mold","q":"Your parents are coming to visit, so you throw away the loaf of bread with the black fuzzy stuff on it. What phylum of fungi did you probably just trash?","ok":"Zygomycota","wrong":["Basidiomycota","Ascomycota","Chytridiomycota"],"ex":"Black bread mold, <i>Rhizopus stolonifer</i>, is a zygomycete.","img":null},"C317":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 31","tag":"Glomeromycota","q":"You have been given the task of finding living members of the phylum Glomeromycota. Where is the best place to look?","ok":"the roots of vascular plants","wrong":["between the toes of someone with athlete's foot","growing on rocks and tree bark","in stagnant freshwater ponds"],"ex":"Glomeromycetes form arbuscular mycorrhizae inside plant roots. Rocks and bark suggest lichens, and ponds suggest chytrids.","img":null},"C318":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 31","tag":"Eight spores","q":"You are given a fungus to identify. It has a fruiting body with many structures containing eight haploid spores lined up in a row. What kind of fungus is this?","ok":"ascomycete","wrong":["zygomycete","chytrid","deuteromycete"],"ex":"Spores in a sac (ascus), usually eight in a row, mean an ascomycete.","img":null},"C319":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 31","tag":"Chytrids","q":"Which feature of the chytrids supports the hypothesis that they represent the most primitive fungi?","ok":"flagellated spores","wrong":["the absence of chitin within the cell wall","parasitic lifestyle","formation of resistant zygosporangia"],"ex":"Fungi descend from a flagellated ancestor, and chytrids are the only fungi that kept flagella. They do have chitin walls, and zygosporangia belong to zygomycetes.","img":null},"C3110":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 31","tag":"Mushroom nuclei","q":"If you analyze the cells of a basidiomycete mushroom, such as the ones we eat, what nuclei would most cells contain?","ok":"two non-identical haploid nuclei","wrong":["one haploid nucleus","two or more genetically identical haploid nuclei","one diploid nucleus","two or more genetically identical diploid nuclei"],"ex":"The fruiting body is made of dikaryotic cells, each with one haploid nucleus from each parent. Karyogamy happens only in the basidia.","img":null},"C3111":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 31","tag":"Fairy ring","q":"In fields or open woodlands you can sometimes find a \"fairy ring\" of mushrooms many meters across. Which is the correct explanation?","ok":"The circle represents the edge of a large underground feeding mycelium that produced fruiting bodies in many places at once.","wrong":["Mushrooms have grown where a circle of spores was deposited previously.","The circle represents the survivors or \"winners\" of many fungi that competed with each other.","The circle represents the outer edge of a good habitat for the mushrooms.","The circle is where fairies have danced in a ring."],"ex":"The mycelium grows outward from a center and uses up food behind it, so mushrooms pop up along the advancing edge.","img":["../drill/img/fairy-ring.jpg","A ring of mushrooms in a lawn"]},"C3112":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 31","tag":"How many fungi?","q":"How many individual fungi are observable in this picture?","ok":"1","wrong":["5","46","52"],"ex":"The whole ring is one mycelium, so all the mushrooms are fruiting bodies of a single individual.","img":["../drill/img/fairy-ring.jpg","A ring of mushrooms in a lawn"]},"C3113":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 31","tag":"Antibiotics","q":"Many fungi produce antibiotics such as penicillin that stop bacterial growth. What is the most likely evolutionary advantage to the fungus?","ok":"competition: destroying bacteria that compete for their food","wrong":["defense: preventing bacteria from infecting the fungus","defense: preventing bacteria from killing fungal spores","symbiosis: attracting helpful bacteria","predatory: the fungus can then consume the bacteria"],"ex":"Bacteria and fungi both decompose the same food. Killing bacteria leaves more for the fungus.","img":null},"C3114":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 31","tag":"Lichens","q":"Lichens are often among the first organisms to grow on bare rock or volcanic deposits. What features of lichens make them successful colonizers?","ok":"All of the above","wrong":["Fungal hyphae have extensive surface area for absorbing runoff water without any soil.","Fungal hyphae secrete weak acids that break down the rock surface and permit attachment.","Some lichen microbes conduct their own nitrogen fixation.","The photosynthetic partners in lichens have little competition for sunlight."],"ex":"Each statement is true. The fungus absorbs water and grips the rock, while the partner makes food and sometimes fixes nitrogen.","img":null},"C390":{"subject":"Signals","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 39","tag":"Acid growth","q":"According to the acid growth hypothesis, what is the correct sequence of these events leading to auxin-induced cell elongation? (1) Cross-links between cellulose microfibrils and other wall polysaccharides are enzymatically broken. (2) Increased turgor pressure expands the cell. (3) Proton pumps move H<sup>+</sup> into the cell wall.","ok":"3, 1, 2","wrong":["1, 2, 3","1, 3, 2","2, 3, 1","3, 2, 1"],"ex":"First, pumps acidify the wall (3). Then low pH activates enzymes that loosen it (1). Then water uptake and turgor stretch the cell (2).","img":["../drill/img/acid-growth.jpg","The acid growth hypothesis: proton pumps acidify the cell wall, expansins loosen it, and the cell elongates"]},"C391":{"subject":"Signals","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 39","tag":"Elongation direction","q":"The cell elongates in one direction, not in every dimension. Which of these could explain that? (1) Proton pumps spread evenly over the membrane are all equally active. (2) Active proton pumps are limited to the part of the membrane parallel to the axis of elongation. (3) Expansins and wall-loosening enzymes are spread evenly outside the membrane. (4) Expansins are limited to the side of the cell perpendicular to the axis of elongation.","ok":"2 only","wrong":["1 only","1 or 3","1 or 4","2 and 4"],"ex":"To stretch in one direction, the wall must loosen only on the sides that run along that direction. Acid pumped only along those sides does it. Even pumping (1) or even enzymes (3) would expand the cell in every direction.","img":["../drill/img/acid-growth.jpg","The acid growth hypothesis: proton pumps acidify the cell wall, expansins loosen it, and the cell elongates"]},"C392":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 39","tag":"Leaf fall","q":"Which of these is most directly involved in causing the leaves of deciduous trees to fall in autumn?","ok":"ethylene","wrong":["cytokinins","gibberellins","abscisic acid","brassinosteroids"],"ex":"A change in the balance of ethylene and auxin triggers the abscission layer at the base of the petiole. The name abscisic acid is misleading: ABA's main jobs are dormancy and drought.","img":null},"C393":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 39","tag":"Under the sidewalk","q":"A patch of soil with ungerminated seeds gets covered by a new concrete sidewalk. If the seeds germinate, which phenomenon will the seedlings show, and which hormone accounts for it?","ok":"triple response; ethylene","wrong":["seed dormancy; abscisic acid","anti-aging effects; cytokinins","anti-aging effects; abscisic acid","triple response; brassinosteroids"],"ex":"Pressing against the concrete makes the seedlings produce ethylene. They slow elongation, thicken, and grow sideways to get around the obstacle.","img":null},"C394":{"subject":"Light & time","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 39","tag":"Phytochrome","q":"Circadian rhythms, seasonal rhythms, and seedling germination are most strongly influenced by a molecular switch involving a phytochrome sensitive to both","ok":"red light and far-red light.","wrong":["violet light and ultraviolet light.","infrared radiation and red light.","blue light and red light.","geomagnetism and cosmic radiation."],"ex":"Phytochrome flips between Pr and Pfr: red light makes Pfr and far-red light makes Pr. Blue light is detected by a different set of receptors.","img":null},"C395":{"subject":"Light & time","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 39","tag":"Long-day flowering","q":"To get a long-day (short-night) plant to flower when nights are longer than its critical night length, the best way would be to interrupt the plant's","ok":"nights with a few minutes of exposure to red light.","wrong":["days with a few minutes of complete darkness during its exposure to light.","nights with a few minutes of exposure to blue light.","nights with a few minutes of exposure to far-red light.","nights with a few minutes of exposure to any color of light."],"ex":"A red flash splits one long night into two short ones, and phytochrome detects it. Far-red would reverse the effect, and a short dark break in the day does nothing because night length is what counts.","img":null},"C396":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 39","tag":"Gravitropism","q":"For plants to sense gravity in their roots, one model proposes they use the distribution of which organelle? What type of gravitropism would roots typically display?","ok":"statoliths; positive gravitropism","wrong":["otoliths; positive gravitropism","statoliths; negative gravitropism","statocysts; negative gravitropism","statocysts; positive gravitropism"],"ex":"Statoliths are starch-filled plastids (amyloplasts) that settle to the low side of root cap cells. Roots grow down, toward gravity (positive); shoots grow up (negative). Otoliths and statocysts are animal structures.","img":null},"C397":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 39","tag":"Temperature stress","q":"In response to temperature extremes, plants may make heat-shock proteins or antifreeze proteins. These protect against which dangers, respectively?","ok":"protein denaturation, ice crystal formation","wrong":["wilting, inability to synthesize hormones","permanent closure of stomata, evapotranspiration","leaf abscission, protein denaturation","evapotranspiration, inability to perform motor activities"],"ex":"Heat-shock proteins help other proteins keep their shape in high heat. Antifreeze proteins stop ice crystals from growing in the cold.","img":null},"C398":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 39","tag":"Pathogens","q":"Plant cells can do all of the following in response to attack by a pathogen except","ok":"create antibodies against specific items found in the pathogen.","wrong":["kill themselves.","synthesize chemicals that inhibit the pathogen.","limit the spread of the pathogen to other cells.","secrete chemicals that signal the presence of pathogens to other parts of the plant."],"ex":"Antibodies belong to the adaptive immunity of vertebrates. Plants rely on innate immunity: the hypersensitive response (including self-sacrifice of cells) and systemic acquired resistance.","img":null},"C399":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 39","tag":"SAR order","q":"What is the correct sequence of these events of systemic acquired resistance? (1) Phloem transports a chemical signal to distant plant parts. (2) Damaged tissues secrete methylsalicylic acid. (3) Salicylic acid induces production of PR proteins. (4) Methylsalicylic acid is converted to salicylic acid in healthy cells.","ok":"2, 1, 4, 3","wrong":["1, 2, 4, 3","1, 3, 4, 2","2, 4, 1, 3","2, 4, 3, 1"],"ex":"Make the signal (2), ship it (1), convert it at the destination (4), and switch on defenses (3).","img":null},"C3910":{"subject":"Signals","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 39","tag":"Growth vs. behavior","q":"Which of the following is a growth response, not a behavioral response?","ok":"elongation of a cell in the root cortex upon auxin-induced cell wall acidification","wrong":["opening of the leaf's stomatal pores during the day when sufficient water is available","leaflet folding by Mimosa pudica upon being touched","\"sleep\" movement of plant leaves at night to align them vertically","closing of stomatal pores when guard cells detect abscisic acid"],"ex":"Growth responses are permanent (new structure or size). The other choices are reversible movements, which count as plant behavior.","img":null},"C3911":{"subject":"Signals","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 39","tag":"De-etiolation","q":"The de-etiolation response (see figure) is a growth response, and not a behavioral response, because","ok":"it involves the formation of new permanent structures and molecules.","wrong":["the response is done upon the detection of light.","there is the involvement of complex transduction mechanisms.","two sets of protein kinases are involved in transduction.","the concentration of cyclic GMP initially rises."],"ex":"Greening builds chloroplasts, chlorophyll and expanded leaves, which are permanent changes. Behaviors like sleep movements can also involve light and kinases, so those choices don't separate the two.","img":["../drill/img/deetiolation.jpg","Signal transduction in de-etiolation: phytochrome, cGMP and Ca2+, protein kinases, transcription factors"]},"C3912":{"subject":"Light & time","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 39","tag":"Canopy light","q":"Leaves at the top of a canopy get direct sun, so their phytochromes are in a high _____ ratio. Shaded leaves at the bottom have more of the _____ form because they receive a higher proportion of _____ light.","ok":"Pfr/Pr; Pr; far-red","wrong":["Pfr/Pr; Pr; red","Pfr/Pr; Pfr; far-red","Pr/Pfr; Pr; far-red","Pfr/Pr; Pfr; red"],"ex":"Leaves above absorb red light and let far-red through, so shade is rich in far-red light, which converts Pfr to Pr. That signals shade and makes plants grow taller to escape it.","img":null},"C3913":{"subject":"Light & time","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 39","tag":"Sleep movements","q":"The \"sleep\" movements of many plants are repeated each day and usually don't form any new permanent structures. These movements are best considered a type of","ok":"plant behavior.","wrong":["response to daily changes in the magnetic field.","developmental response.","response to daily changes in water stress experienced by the plant.","defense against herbivory at night."],"ex":"Reversible, repeated movements without new structures count as behavior. Bean leaves rise and fall on a circadian rhythm even in constant conditions.","img":null},"C3914":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 39","tag":"Signals & responses","q":"Which choice INCORRECTLY matches an environmental signal with a typical response in vascular plants?","ok":"flooding of the soil, production of abscisic acid in the shoots","wrong":["high osmolarity of the soil solution, production of osmotically compensating solutes","gravity, negative gravitropic growth by shoots","exposure of lettuce seeds to red light, promotion of seed germination","high temperatures in the leaves, production of heat-shock proteins"],"ex":"Flooding triggers ethylene and the formation of air tubes in the roots. ABA production is the response to drought.","img":null},"C3915":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 39","tag":"Innate immunity","q":"A common feature of the innate immune systems of many eukaryotes is the presence of","ok":"receptors specific for pathogen-associated molecular structures.","wrong":["phagocytotic cells that destroy pathogens.","disease resistance (R) genes.","enzymes that produce methyl salicylic acid.","expression of genes coding for factors needed for systemic acquired resistance."],"ex":"Plants and animals both recognize molecular patterns shared by pathogens (like bacterial flagellin). R genes and salicylic acid are plant-specific, and phagocytes are animal-specific.","img":null},"C3916":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Clicker questions · Ch 39","tag":"SAR cost","q":"Systemic acquired resistance lasts several days. If it were kept always on, one likely result would be","ok":"reduced growth rate of the plant, due to the metabolic costs of deploying this system of resistance.","wrong":["the accumulation of methyl salicylic acid in the roots of the plant.","higher rates of infection by other types of pathogens.","the spread of more pathogenic effectors into the plant tissues.","the production of more gibberellins to promote stem elongation."],"ex":"Defense is expensive. Energy and materials spent on PR proteins can't go into growth, which is why the plant switches SAR on only when needed.","img":null},"LS0":{"subject":"Signals","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"Dodder","q":"A dodder (parasitic vine) seedling bends toward a nearby tomato plant. What is it responding to?","ok":"Chemicals released by the host plant","wrong":["Light reflected off the host","Gravity","Touch from the host's leaves"],"ex":"Plants can't move to a better spot, so they respond by changing how they grow. Dodder grows toward the volatile chemicals its host releases.","img":null},"LS1":{"subject":"Signals","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"Darwin experiments","q":"In the Darwins' experiments, which coleoptile still bent toward light?","ok":"One with an opaque shield covering the bending region but the tip exposed","wrong":["One with its tip removed","One with an opaque cap over the tip","None of them bent"],"ex":"The tip senses the light. Covering the tip stops bending, but covering the region that bends doesn't.","img":null},"LS2":{"subject":"Signals","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"Boysen-Jensen","q":"Boysen-Jensen separated coleoptile tips from the rest with gelatin (permeable) or mica (impermeable). What did the result show?","ok":"The signal is a mobile chemical, since bending happened only through gelatin","wrong":["The signal is electrical, since bending happened only through mica","The tip isn't needed for bending","Light travels down the coleoptile like a fiber optic"],"ex":"A chemical can diffuse through gelatin but not mica.","img":null},"LS3":{"subject":"Signals","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"Went","q":"Frits Went placed coleoptile tips on agar blocks, then put the blocks off-center on decapitated coleoptiles. What happened?","ok":"The coleoptiles curved away from the side with the block, because the chemical (auxin) made that side grow more","wrong":["The coleoptiles curved toward the block","Nothing happened, because the agar blocked the signal","The coleoptiles shrank"],"ex":"The block side gets more auxin and grows faster, so the shoot curves away from it, even in darkness. Plain agar blocks (the control) did nothing.","img":null},"LS4":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"Auxin","q":"What is the most common natural auxin in plants?","ok":"Indoleacetic acid (IAA)","wrong":["Abscisic acid (ABA)","Salicylic acid","Gibberellic acid"],"ex":"\"Auxin\" means any chemical that promotes coleoptile elongation; IAA is the one plants make most.","img":null},"LS5":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"Hormones","q":"Which statement about plant hormones is true?","ok":"They act at very low concentrations, each has several effects, and several hormones can influence one process","wrong":["Each hormone controls exactly one process","They act only at high concentrations","They are produced only in the flowers"],"ex":"Plant responses usually depend on the combination and the concentrations of hormones present.","img":null},"LS6":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"Crown gall","q":"<i>Agrobacterium</i> causes crown gall tumors in plants. How?","ok":"It inserts genes that raise the plant's cytokinin and auxin production, causing massive cell division","wrong":["It makes the plant produce too much ethylene","It destroys the plant's ABA","It blocks the phloem"],"ex":"Extra cytokinin and auxin drive uncontrolled division: a tumor.","img":null},"LS7":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"Apical dominance","q":"A gardener pinches off the terminal bud of a plant. What happens?","ok":"Axillary buds grow into lateral branches, so the plant becomes bushier","wrong":["The plant stops growing completely","The plant grows a taller main stem","The plant flowers immediately"],"ex":"The terminal bud suppresses axillary buds (apical dominance). Adding auxin to the cut stump restores the suppression.","img":null},"LS8":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"Foolish seedlings","q":"Gibberellins were named after the fungus <i>Gibberella fujikuroi</i>. What did the fungus do to rice?","ok":"Made infected rice plants grow very tall and spindly","wrong":["Made rice plants dwarfed","Made rice ripen early","Made rice seeds stay dormant"],"ex":"The fungus releases gibberellins, which cause excessive stem elongation. Adding gibberellins to certain dwarf mutants restores normal height.","img":null},"LS9":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"Gibberellins","q":"Besides stem and fruit growth, gibberellins also","ok":"hasten seed germination, and in ferns they act as pheromones","wrong":["close stomata in drought","cause the triple response","make leaves fall"],"ex":"In many seeds, gibberellins signal the embryo to break dormancy and use its stored food.","img":null},"LS10":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"Precocious germination","q":"Red mangrove seeds and certain maize mutants germinate while still on the parent plant. What explains this?","ok":"Low or inactive ABA, so nothing maintains dormancy","wrong":["Too much ABA","Too much ethylene","Too much cytokinin"],"ex":"The maize mutants lack a transcription factor needed for ABA to switch on its genes, so the seeds germinate early (precocious germination).","img":null},"LS11":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"Ethylene triggers","q":"Plants produce ethylene in response to","ok":"stresses such as drought, flooding, mechanical pressure, injury and infection","wrong":["only bright light","only cold","only nightfall"],"ex":"Ethylene is a stress hormone. It also drives fruit ripening, senescence and leaf abscission.","img":null},"LS12":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"Triple-response mutants","q":"An Arabidopsis <i>ein</i> mutant is exposed to ethylene. What happens?","ok":"Nothing: it is ethylene-insensitive and does not show the triple response","wrong":["It shows an extra-strong triple response","It flowers right away","It drops its leaves"],"ex":"<i>ein</i> stands for ethylene-insensitive. The <i>ctr</i> mutants show the triple response even without added ethylene.","img":null},"LS13":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"Ripening","q":"How have tomatoes been engineered to stay fresh longer?","ok":"An antisense copy of an ethylene-synthesis gene blocks the ethylene burst that triggers ripening","wrong":["Extra gibberellin genes were added","ABA was removed from the fruit","Cytokinin was sprayed on the fruit"],"ex":"No ethylene burst means slow ripening. Growers also pick fruit green and control the ethylene levels around it.","img":null},"LS14":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"Senescence","q":"Senescence is","ok":"the programmed death of cells or organs, associated with a burst of ethylene","wrong":["the greening of a dark-grown shoot","seed dormancy","the bending of a shoot toward light"],"ex":"Senescence (programmed cell death) is behind leaf aging, petal drop and fruit ripening.","img":null},"LS15":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"Jasmonates & strigolactones","q":"Which hormones mediate defenses against insect herbivores (jasmonates), and which regulate apical dominance, seed germination and mycorrhizal associations (strigolactones)?","ok":"Jasmonates for herbivore defense; strigolactones for branching, germination and mycorrhizae","wrong":["Strigolactones for herbivore defense; jasmonates for branching","Both only control fruit ripening","Both only control stomata"],"ex":"These are the two hormones from the slide's hormone summary table that the HW didn't ask about.","img":null},"LS16":{"subject":"Light & time","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"Photomorphogenesis","q":"What is photomorphogenesis?","ok":"The permanent effects of light on plant form and development","wrong":["Movement toward light","Making sugar from light","The daily rhythm of leaf movement"],"ex":"Phototropism is the bending toward light. Photomorphogenesis is the broader set of light effects on form, such as de-etiolation.","img":null},"LS17":{"subject":"Light & time","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"Light receptors","q":"What do blue-light photoreceptors control?","ok":"Hypocotyl elongation, stomatal opening, and phototropism","wrong":["Seed germination and shade avoidance","Fruit ripening","Leaf abscission"],"ex":"There are two major classes of light receptors. Blue-light receptors control the responses here; phytochromes control germination and shade avoidance.","img":null},"LS18":{"subject":"Light & time","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"Lettuce seeds","q":"In the classic USDA lettuce-seed experiments, which light promoted germination and which inhibited it?","ok":"Red light promoted it; far-red light inhibited it","wrong":["Far-red light promoted it; red light inhibited it","Blue light promoted it; red light inhibited it","Light had no effect"],"ex":"The effects are reversible, and the last flash decides.","img":null},"LS19":{"subject":"Light & time","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"Circadian clock","q":"Which is NOT one of the four characteristics of circadian rhythms?","ok":"They stop as soon as the plant is put in constant darkness","wrong":["They continue without external cues","They last about 24 hours","They can be reset (entrained)"],"ex":"Circadian rhythms keep going in constant conditions; that's how we know they come from an internal clock. The fourth characteristic is that the clock compensates for temperature.","img":null},"LS20":{"subject":"Light & time","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"Night length","q":"In the 1940s, researchers found that flowering in photoperiodic plants is actually controlled by","ok":"the length of the night","wrong":["the length of the day","the temperature","the amount of rainfall"],"ex":"Breaking up the night with a flash of light changes flowering; breaking up the day with darkness doesn't.","img":null},"LS21":{"subject":"Light & time","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"Red, then far-red","q":"A short-day plant's long night is interrupted by a flash of red light immediately followed by far-red. What happens?","ok":"It still flowers: the far-red reverses the red flash, so the night counts as unbroken","wrong":["It doesn't flower, because any light breaks the night","It dies","It flowers twice"],"ex":"The last flash decides. Far-red after red cancels the interruption. This photoreversibility shows phytochrome is the pigment that detects the flash.","img":null},"LS22":{"subject":"Light & time","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"Vernalization","q":"What is vernalization?","ok":"A cold pretreatment that some plants need before they will flower","wrong":["A red-light flash at night","Removing the terminal bud","Soaking seeds to remove ABA"],"ex":"Some plants need an environmental stimulus in addition to the right photoperiod. Winter wheat, for example, must go through a cold period before it flowers.","img":null},"LS23":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"Statoliths","q":"What are statoliths?","ok":"Plastids (amyloplasts) with dense starch grains that settle to the bottom of root cap cells","wrong":["Calcium crystals in the leaves","Hormone-producing glands","Fungal spores in the roots"],"ex":"Their settling tells the cell which way is down.","img":null},"LS24":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"Mimosa","q":"How does <i>Mimosa pudica</i> fold its leaflets so quickly when touched?","ok":"Electrical impulses (action potentials) trigger rapid loss of turgor in motor cells","wrong":["New cells grow on one side","Ethylene gas is released","The leaflets die and fall"],"ex":"This rapid turgor movement is reversible behavior, not growth.","img":null},"LS25":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"Pathogen entry","q":"How do pathogens usually get past a plant's first line of defense?","ok":"Through wounds or natural openings such as stomata","wrong":["Through intact epidermis by digging","Through the xylem from the soil","Only through flowers"],"ex":"The epidermis and periderm are the first barrier. Once a pathogen is inside, chemical defenses take over.","img":null},"LS26":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Practice, from the lecture slides","tag":"R proteins","q":"In effector-triggered immunity, how does a plant recognize a pathogen?","ok":"Its R (resistance) proteins detect specific effector proteins that the pathogen injects","wrong":["It makes antibodies against the pathogen","Its phagocytes engulf the pathogen","It detects the pathogen with phytochrome"],"ex":"R gene products recognize effectors and set off the hypersensitive response and systemic acquired resistance.","img":null},"AP0":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Going Green on Land","q":"Land plants evolved from this group of green algae.","ok":"Charophytes","wrong":["Red algae","Diatoms","Brown algae"],"ex":"Charophytes share sporopollenin, cellulose-making rosettes and similar sperm with land plants.","img":null},"AP1":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Going Green on Land","q":"Liverworts, mosses and hornworts are grouped together under this name.","ok":"Bryophytes","wrong":["Lycophytes","Gymnosperms","Pterophytes"],"ex":"Bryophytes are the nonvascular plants.","img":null},"AP2":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Going Green on Land","q":"In a moss, this generation is the green leafy plant you see.","ok":"The gametophyte (n)","wrong":["The sporophyte (2n)","The zygote","The spore"],"ex":"The gametophyte dominates in bryophytes.","img":null},"AP3":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Going Green on Land","q":"Multicellular sex organs that produce eggs.","ok":"Archegonia","wrong":["Antheridia","Sporangia","Sori"],"ex":"Archegonia make eggs, and antheridia make sperm.","img":null},"AP4":{"subject":"Seedless vascular","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Going Green on Land","q":"Ferns and lycophytes still need this for fertilization.","ok":"Water, so flagellated sperm can swim to the egg","wrong":["Pollen","Wind","Animals"],"ex":"Swimming sperm tie seedless plants to moist habitats.","img":null},"AP5":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Seeds & Flowers","q":"A pollen grain is this generation, packaged in a tough wall.","ok":"The male gametophyte","wrong":["The male sporophyte","A sperm cell","A seed"],"ex":"Pollen develops from a microspore.","img":null},"AP6":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Seeds & Flowers","q":"\"Naked seed\" plants, such as pines.","ok":"Gymnosperms","wrong":["Angiosperms","Bryophytes","Ferns"],"ex":"Gymno- means naked.","img":null},"AP7":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Seeds & Flowers","q":"A fruit is a mature version of this flower part.","ok":"The ovary","wrong":["The ovule","The anther","The petal"],"ex":"Ovule → seed; ovary → fruit.","img":null},"AP8":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Seeds & Flowers","q":"This triploid tissue feeds the angiosperm embryo.","ok":"Endosperm","wrong":["Female gametophyte","Seed coat","Cotyledon sheath"],"ex":"It forms when a sperm fuses with the two polar nuclei.","img":null},"AP9":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Seeds & Flowers","q":"Making two kinds of spores, microspores and megaspores.","ok":"Heterospory","wrong":["Homospory","Double fertilization","Alternation of generations"],"ex":"All seed plants are heterosporous.","img":null},"AP10":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Hormone Hotline","q":"This gas ripens fruit.","ok":"Ethylene","wrong":["Auxin","Cytokinin","Gibberellin"],"ex":"One ripening fruit can speed the ripening of others nearby.","img":null},"AP11":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Hormone Hotline","q":"This hormone closes stomata during drought.","ok":"Abscisic acid (ABA)","wrong":["Ethylene","Auxin","Brassinosteroids"],"ex":"ABA is the main drought signal.","img":null},"AP12":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Hormone Hotline","q":"This hormone class stimulates cytokinesis and slows aging.","ok":"Cytokinins","wrong":["Gibberellins","Jasmonates","Abscisic acid"],"ex":"Cytokinins also promote lateral bud growth.","img":null},"AP13":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Hormone Hotline","q":"Growers spray these on Thompson seedless grapes to make them bigger.","ok":"Gibberellins","wrong":["Ethylene","Abscisic acid","Strigolactones"],"ex":"Gibberellins lengthen internodes and boost fruit growth.","img":null},"AP14":{"subject":"Hormones","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Hormone Hotline","q":"These hormones are chemically similar to animal sex hormones.","ok":"Brassinosteroids","wrong":["Jasmonates","Auxins","Cytokinins"],"ex":"They are steroids that induce cell elongation and division.","img":null},"AP15":{"subject":"Light & time","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Light Switch","q":"The red/far-red light receptor.","ok":"Phytochrome","wrong":["Chlorophyll","Cryptochrome","Statolith"],"ex":"Phytochrome switches between Pr and Pfr.","img":null},"AP16":{"subject":"Light & time","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Light Switch","q":"Red light converts Pr into this active form.","ok":"Pfr","wrong":["Pr","cGMP","IAA"],"ex":"Far-red light converts it back.","img":null},"AP17":{"subject":"Light & time","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Light Switch","q":"Rhythms of about 24 hours.","ok":"Circadian rhythms","wrong":["Photoperiodism","Vernalization","Senescence"],"ex":"They continue even without external cues.","img":null},"AP18":{"subject":"Light & time","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Light Switch","q":"Short-day plants really measure the length of this.","ok":"The night","wrong":["The day","The temperature","The rainy season"],"ex":"They flower when the night is longer than the critical night length.","img":null},"AP19":{"subject":"Signals","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Light Switch","q":"The pale, long-stemmed growth of a sprout in the dark.","ok":"Etiolation","wrong":["De-etiolation","Phototropism","Thigmomorphogenesis"],"ex":"De-etiolation (greening) reverses it once light arrives.","img":null},"AP20":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Under Attack","q":"Plant cells sacrifice themselves around an infection site.","ok":"The hypersensitive response","wrong":["Systemic acquired resistance","The triple response","Apical dominance"],"ex":"It walls off the pathogen.","img":null},"AP21":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Under Attack","q":"This acid, sent through the phloem as its methyl form, triggers systemic acquired resistance.","ok":"Salicylic acid","wrong":["Abscisic acid","Indoleacetic acid","Jasmonic acid"],"ex":"It is converted to salicylic acid in distant healthy cells.","img":null},"AP22":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Under Attack","q":"Heat makes plants produce these protective proteins.","ok":"Heat-shock proteins","wrong":["Antifreeze proteins","PR proteins","Expansins"],"ex":"They help keep other proteins from denaturing.","img":null},"AP23":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Under Attack","q":"Maize under caterpillar attack recruits these insects.","ok":"Parasitoid wasps","wrong":["Honeybees","Ladybugs that eat leaves","Fruit flies"],"ex":"The wasps lay their eggs in the caterpillars.","img":null},"AP24":{"subject":"Stress & defense","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Under Attack","q":"Plants lack these, a key part of vertebrate adaptive immunity.","ok":"Antibodies","wrong":["Receptors for pathogen molecules","R genes","Cell walls"],"ex":"Plants rely on innate immunity only.","img":null},"AP25":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Fun-gi","q":"The tangled mass of hyphae that forms a fungus's body.","ok":"The mycelium","wrong":["The fruiting body","The ascus","The basidium"],"ex":"Mushrooms are only its reproductive parts.","img":null},"AP26":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Fun-gi","q":"The tough polymer in fungal cell walls.","ok":"Chitin","wrong":["Cellulose","Lignin","Sporopollenin"],"ex":"Chitin is also in insect exoskeletons.","img":null},"AP27":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Fun-gi","q":"The only fungi with flagellated spores.","ok":"Chytrids","wrong":["Zygomycetes","Ascomycetes","Basidiomycetes"],"ex":"That's why they are thought to be the earliest-diverging fungi.","img":null},"AP28":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Fun-gi","q":"The fusion of two nuclei in a fungal life cycle.","ok":"Karyogamy","wrong":["Plasmogamy","Meiosis","Budding"],"ex":"Plasmogamy is the fusion of cytoplasm, which comes first.","img":null},"AP29":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Practice: Bio-pardy review game, from the lecture slides","tag":"Fun-gi","q":"A fungus living together with a green alga or cyanobacterium.","ok":"A lichen","wrong":["A mycorrhiza","An endophyte","A heterokaryon"],"ex":"Mycorrhizae are fungus–root partnerships.","img":null}}},"hw3":{"name":"HW 3","key":"bio-hw3-v1","html":true,"href":"../hw3/index.html","items":{"HW0":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q1","q":"What are the closest living relatives of land plants?","ok":"Charophytes (a group of green algae)","wrong":["Red algae (rhodophytes)","Brown algae (phaeophytes)","Diatoms (bacillariophytes)"],"ex":"Charophytes are the green algae most closely related to land plants. Both DNA comparisons and shared cell features (cellulose-making rosettes, sperm structure, sporopollenin) point to them.","img":null},"HW1":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q2 (advantages)","q":"When plants first colonized land, which conditions were advantages?","ok":"Unfiltered sunlight, more CO<sub>2</sub>, nutrient-rich soil, and at first few herbivores or pathogens","wrong":["Water all around the body, so there was no risk of drying out","The buoyancy of water to hold the body up","An easy route for swimming sperm to reach eggs"],"ex":"On land, light isn't filtered by water, CO<sub>2</sub> is more plentiful in air, soil holds minerals, and early on there were almost no animals to eat plants or microbes to infect them.","img":null},"HW2":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q2 (problems)","q":"Which were the main problems early land plants had to overcome?","ok":"Drying out (scarce water) and no water to support the body against gravity","wrong":["Too little sunlight for photosynthesis","Too little CO<sub>2</sub> in the air","Constant attack by herbivores from the start"],"ex":"Out of the water, plants lose water to the air and have nothing buoyant to hold them up. Adaptations like the cuticle, stomata, vascular tissue and lignin solve these problems.","img":null},"HW3":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q3 (shared traits)","q":"Which traits do land plants share with charophytes?","ok":"Ring-shaped (rosette) cellulose-synthesizing complexes, similar flagellated sperm, and sporopollenin that protects the zygote from drying","wrong":["Vascular tissue, stomata and a waxy cuticle","Alternation of generations with a dominant sporophyte","Seeds, pollen and flowers"],"ex":"These three features are found only in land plants and charophytes. Sporopollenin, a tough polymer, keeps charophyte zygotes from drying out at the edges of ponds. Its use in spore walls is part of what let plants move onto land.","img":null},"HW4":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q3 (what's different)","q":"Which traits distinguish land plants (embryophytes) from charophytes?","ok":"Alternation of generations with dependent embryos, walled spores made in sporangia, multicellular gametangia, and apical meristems","wrong":["Chlorophyll <i>a</i> and <i>b</i>, cellulose cell walls and starch storage","Flagellated sperm and sporopollenin","Rosette cellulose-synthesizing complexes"],"ex":"These are the derived traits of land plants. Chlorophyll <i>a</i> and <i>b</i>, cellulose walls and starch are shared with green algae, and the other wrong answers are shared with charophytes, so none of them separate the two groups.","img":null},"HW5":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q4 (classified by)","q":"Plants can be classified based on the presence or absence of","ok":"vascular tissue","wrong":["chlorophyll","cell walls","alternation of generations"],"ex":"Vascular tissue (xylem and phloem) divides plants into nonvascular plants (bryophytes) and vascular plants. Every land plant has chlorophyll, cell walls and alternation of generations.","img":null},"HW6":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q4 (nonvascular)","q":"Nonvascular plants are called","ok":"bryophytes (liverworts, mosses and hornworts)","wrong":["lycophytes","pterophytes (ferns)","gymnosperms"],"ex":"Bryophytes are the nonvascular plants. Lycophytes and ferns are seedless vascular plants, and gymnosperms are seed plants.","img":null},"HW7":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q5","q":"Which generation dominates the life cycle of nonvascular plants, and which dominates in vascular plants?","ok":"Haploid gametophyte in nonvascular plants; diploid sporophyte in vascular plants","wrong":["Diploid sporophyte in nonvascular plants; haploid gametophyte in vascular plants","Sporophyte in both","Gametophyte in both"],"ex":"In a moss, the green leafy plant you see is the gametophyte (n), and the small sporophyte (2n) grows from it and depends on it. In ferns, conifers and flowering plants, the large plant is the sporophyte.","img":["../drill/img/moss-cycle.jpg","The moss life cycle: haploid gametophytes (n) and the diploid sporophyte (2n) growing from them"]},"HW8":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q6","q":"Which set of characteristics is specific to vascular plants?","ok":"A dominant sporophyte, xylem and phloem reinforced with lignin, and well-developed roots and leaves","wrong":["A dominant gametophyte and flagellated sperm","Seeds and flowers","Stomata and a cuticle"],"ex":"Vascular plants have life cycles dominated by the sporophyte, transport tissue (xylem and phloem), and true roots and leaves. Seeds and flowers appear only in seed plants. Bryophytes also have a cuticle, and mosses and hornworts have stomata.","img":null},"HW9":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q6 (xylem & phloem)","q":"In vascular tissue, what do xylem and phloem each carry?","ok":"Xylem carries water and minerals (through dead, lignified cells); phloem carries sugars and other organic products (through living cells)","wrong":["Xylem carries sugars; phloem carries water and minerals","Both carry only water","Xylem carries gametes; phloem carries spores"],"ex":"Xylem's tracheids are dead cells with lignin-reinforced walls, which also hold the plant up. Phloem is living tissue that distributes sugars, amino acids and other organic products.","img":null},"HW10":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q6 (height)","q":"What did lignified vascular tissue allow vascular plants to do that bryophytes cannot?","ok":"Grow tall, since it gives structural support and moves water over long distances","wrong":["Reproduce by spores","Make chlorophyll","Live without stomata"],"ex":"Lignin stiffens xylem walls, so stems could hold plants up and carry water high. Taller plants win the competition for light and spread their spores farther. This matches a clicker question in Ch 29.","img":null},"HW11":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q7 (nonvascular gametophyte)","q":"Describe the gametophyte of a nonvascular plant such as a moss.","ok":"It is the large, green, long-lived photosynthetic plant; the sporophyte grows from it and depends on it for food","wrong":["It is microscopic and lives inside the sporophyte","It is a small heart-shaped plant that dies once the sporophyte grows","It is a pollen grain carried by the wind"],"ex":"In bryophytes the gametophyte is the dominant generation, and the sporophyte stays attached to it and is nutritionally dependent on it.","img":["../drill/img/moss-cycle.jpg","The moss life cycle: haploid gametophytes (n) and the diploid sporophyte (2n) growing from them"]},"HW12":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q7 (seedless vascular gametophyte)","q":"Describe the gametophyte of a seedless vascular plant such as a fern.","ok":"It is small, free-living and photosynthetic, independent of the large sporophyte","wrong":["It is the large leafy plant, and the sporophyte depends on it","It is retained inside an ovule","It is a pollen grain"],"ex":"A fern gametophyte is a tiny heart-shaped plant (prothallus) on the soil. The young sporophyte starts out on it, but it soon grows independent and becomes the large fern.","img":null},"HW13":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q7 (fertilization)","q":"How does fertilization happen in both bryophytes and seedless vascular plants?","ok":"Flagellated sperm swim through a film of water to the egg in an archegonium","wrong":["Pollen carried by wind delivers sperm to the ovule","Two sperm travel down a pollen tube (double fertilization)","Spores fuse with eggs directly"],"ex":"Both groups depend on water for fertilization: sperm from an antheridium swim to the egg in an archegonium. That is why mosses and ferns are usually found in moist places.","img":null},"HW14":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q7 (moss spores)","q":"In a moss, which structure produces and disperses the spores?","ok":"The sporangium (capsule) at the tip of the sporophyte's stalk (seta)","wrong":["Sori on the underside of leaves","Strobili (cones)","The antheridium"],"ex":"The moss sporophyte is a foot, a stalk (seta) and a capsule (sporangium). Meiosis in the capsule makes spores, which are released when the lid comes off; the peristome teeth control how they are dispersed.","img":["../drill/img/moss-cycle.jpg","The moss life cycle: haploid gametophytes (n) and the diploid sporophyte (2n) growing from them"]},"HW15":{"subject":"Land plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q7 (fern spores)","q":"In a fern, which structure produces and disperses the spores?","ok":"Sporangia grouped in sori on the underside of the sporophyte's leaves (sporophylls)","wrong":["A capsule on a stalk growing from the gametophyte","The archegonium","The ovule"],"ex":"Fern sporangia cluster in sori (singular: sorus) on sporophylls. In lycophytes, sporophylls are grouped into cone-like strobili. Both are on the sporophyte, the dominant generation.","img":null},"HW16":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q8 (seedless vs. seed)","q":"Which describes a key difference between seedless and seed-producing vascular plants?","ok":"Seed plants have tiny gametophytes kept inside the sporophyte and use pollen, so they don't need water for fertilization; seedless plants have free-living gametophytes and swimming sperm","wrong":["Seedless plants have no vascular tissue","Seed plants have a dominant gametophyte","Seedless plants are heterosporous and seed plants are homosporous"],"ex":"Seed plants keep their microscopic gametophytes inside the sporophyte, where they are protected from drying, and deliver sperm in pollen. Seedless vascular plants have free-living gametophytes and swimming sperm and disperse by spores.","img":null},"HW17":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q8 (pollen)","q":"What is a pollen grain?","ok":"A male gametophyte enclosed in a tough pollen wall that contains sporopollenin","wrong":["A sperm cell","A male sporophyte","A spore that grows into the female gametophyte"],"ex":"A microspore develops into a pollen grain: the male gametophyte inside a protective coat. Pollen can travel by wind or animals, so seed plants don't need water to bring sperm to eggs.","img":null},"HW18":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q8 (gametangia)","q":"Where are the gametangia (antheridia and archegonia) located?","ok":"On the haploid gametophyte; in seed plants, the gametophytes are tiny and stay inside the sporophyte","wrong":["On the sporophyte's sporangia","Inside the seed coat of every plant","In sori on the underside of fern leaves"],"ex":"Gametangia are part of the gametophyte: antheridia make sperm and archegonia make eggs. Sori contain sporangia, which make spores, not gametes.","img":null},"HW19":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q8 (heterosporous)","q":"What does it mean for a plant to be heterosporous?","ok":"It makes two kinds of spores: megaspores that become female gametophytes and microspores that become male gametophytes","wrong":["It makes spores by both mitosis and meiosis","It makes both spores and seeds","Its megaspores become male gametophytes"],"ex":"Heterospory means two spore types. Every seed plant is heterosporous; most seedless plants are homosporous, with one kind of spore that grows into a bisexual gametophyte. (The clicker question in Ch 29 tests the mix-up: megaspores make <i>female</i> gametophytes.)","img":null},"HW20":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q9 (seeds)","q":"How do gymnosperms and angiosperms differ in where their seeds develop?","ok":"Gymnosperm seeds are \"naked\" on the scales of cones; angiosperm seeds are enclosed in an ovary that becomes a fruit","wrong":["Gymnosperm seeds are inside fruits; angiosperm seeds are naked","Only angiosperms make seeds","Both enclose their seeds in ovaries"],"ex":"Gymno- means naked: the ovules sit exposed on sporophylls (cone scales). Angio- means vessel: the ovules are enclosed in a carpel's ovary, which matures into fruit.","img":null},"HW21":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q9 (food supply)","q":"How does the food supply of a gymnosperm seed differ from that of an angiosperm seed?","ok":"Gymnosperms: haploid female gametophyte tissue; angiosperms: triploid (3n) endosperm from double fertilization","wrong":["Gymnosperms: triploid endosperm; angiosperms: haploid gametophyte tissue","Both are fed by soil nutrients through the seed coat","Both are fed by the parent's phloem after the seed falls"],"ex":"Only angiosperms have double fertilization, which makes endosperm (3n). In a pine seed, the embryo feeds on the female gametophyte (n).","img":null},"HW22":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q9 (features)","q":"Which features are found in angiosperms but not gymnosperms?","ok":"Flowers, fruits and double fertilization","wrong":["Pollen, ovules and seeds","Heterospory and reduced gametophytes","Vascular tissue and a dominant sporophyte"],"ex":"Pollen, ovules, seeds, heterospory and reduced gametophytes are shared by all seed plants. Flowers, fruits and double fertilization belong to angiosperms alone.","img":null},"HW23":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q10","q":"What is a seed made of?","ok":"An embryo, a food supply, and a protective seed coat","wrong":["A spore, a sporangium and a cuticle","A megaspore, pollen and the ovary wall","A male gametophyte and endosperm only"],"ex":"A seed is a sporophyte embryo packaged with stored food, inside a seed coat that develops from the ovule's integuments. The clicker question on the ancient date palm seed hinges on this stored food.","img":null},"HW24":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q11 (adaptations)","q":"Besides seeds, which adaptations do all seed plants share?","ok":"Reduced gametophytes, heterospory, ovules and pollen","wrong":["Flowers, fruits and double fertilization","Free-living gametophytes and swimming sperm","Homospory and sori"],"ex":"These are the five derived traits of seed plants: seeds, reduced gametophytes, heterospory, ovules and pollen. Flowers, fruits and double fertilization are angiosperm-only.","img":null},"HW25":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q11 (why seeds win)","q":"Why are seeds an advantage over spores?","ok":"A seed holds a multicellular embryo with stored food in a protective coat, so it can stay dormant and wait for good conditions","wrong":["Seeds are single cells, so they spread more easily","Seeds photosynthesize on their own","Seeds don't need a sporophyte parent"],"ex":"A spore is a single cell. A seed already contains an embryo, food and a coat, so it can stay dormant for days to years and germinate when conditions are favorable (see the seed dormancy clicker question).","img":null},"HW26":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q11 (pine life cycle)","q":"In the life cycle of a pine (a gymnosperm), which statement is correct?","ok":"Pollen cones make microspores that become pollen; ovulate cones hold ovules, where a megaspore becomes the female gametophyte","wrong":["The pine tree is the gametophyte generation","Sperm swim through water to reach the ovulate cone","Fertilization happens the moment pollen lands on the cone"],"ex":"The tree is the sporophyte. Pollen lands on an ovule, and a pollen tube grows slowly; fertilization usually happens more than a year after pollination. The zygote becomes an embryo inside a naked seed on the cone scale.","img":null},"HW27":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q11 (angiosperm life cycle)","q":"What does the female gametophyte (embryo sac) of an angiosperm contain?","ok":"An egg, two synergids, three antipodal cells, and a central cell with two polar nuclei","wrong":["Four haploid eggs","One egg and two sperm","A pollen tube and a tube nucleus"],"ex":"One of the four megaspores survives and divides to form the embryo sac. A pollen tube enters through the micropyle and delivers two sperm; one synergid dies as the tube arrives.","img":null},"HW28":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q11 (double fertilization)","q":"In double fertilization, what does each of the two sperm do?","ok":"One fuses with the egg to make the 2n zygote; the other fuses with the two polar nuclei to make 3n endosperm","wrong":["Both fertilize the egg","One fertilizes the egg and the other fertilizes a synergid","One fertilizes the egg and the other guides the pollen tube"],"ex":"This is why angiosperm seeds contain both diploid (embryo) and triploid (endosperm) cells. Afterward the ovule becomes a seed and the ovary becomes a fruit.","img":null},"HW29":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q12 (function of flowers)","q":"What is the function of flowers?","ok":"Sexual reproduction: they make the gametophytes and, in many species, attract pollinators","wrong":["Photosynthesis","Storing food for the embryo","Absorbing water from the air"],"ex":"A flower is the angiosperm structure specialized for sexual reproduction. Animal pollinators carry pollen more directly than wind; wind-pollinated flowers tend to be small, green and inconspicuous.","img":null},"HW30":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q12 (origin of flower parts)","q":"Where do the parts of a flower come from, in evolutionary terms?","ok":"They are modified leaves: sepals, petals, stamens and carpels form four rings (whorls) on a compressed shoot","wrong":["They are modified roots","They are fused gametophytes","They are modified fruits"],"ex":"A flower is a specialized shoot with up to four whorls of modified leaves. Stamens and carpels are sporophylls, the modified leaves that bear sporangia.","img":null},"HW31":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q12 (flower parts)","q":"Which list names the parts of a carpel?","ok":"Stigma, style and ovary (with ovules inside)","wrong":["Anther and filament","Sepal and petal","Anther, style and ovule"],"ex":"The carpel is the female part: a sticky stigma catches pollen, the style leads down to the ovary, and the ovary holds the ovules. A stamen (anther and filament) is the male part, and its anther makes pollen.","img":null},"HW32":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q13 (fruit)","q":"What is a fruit?","ok":"A mature ovary (sometimes with other flower parts)","wrong":["A mature ovule","A mature anther","A fertilized egg"],"ex":"After fertilization, ovules become seeds and the ovary wall thickens into a fruit. A mature ovule is a seed, not a fruit.","img":null},"HW33":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q13 (fitness)","q":"How do fruits add to the fitness of angiosperms?","ok":"They protect the seeds and help disperse them by wind, water or animals","wrong":["They photosynthesize to feed the seed","They attract pollinators","They provide the 3n endosperm"],"ex":"Fruits carry seeds away from the parent: winged fruits ride the wind, burrs hitch rides on fur, and fleshy fruits are eaten and the seeds deposited elsewhere. Flowers attract pollinators; fruits disperse seeds.","img":null},"HW34":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q14 (monocots)","q":"Which set of traits describes a monocot?","ok":"One cotyledon, parallel leaf veins, scattered vascular bundles, fibrous roots, flower parts in threes","wrong":["Two cotyledons, net-like veins, vascular bundles in a ring, a taproot, flower parts in fours or fives","One cotyledon, net-like veins, a taproot, flower parts in fives","No cotyledons, no veins, flower parts in twos"],"ex":"Monocots include grasses, lilies, orchids and palms. Eudicots have the opposite set of traits.","img":null},"HW35":{"subject":"Seed plants","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q14 (eudicots)","q":"A plant has two cotyledons, net-like leaf veins, vascular bundles in a ring, a taproot and flowers with parts in fives. It is a","ok":"eudicot","wrong":["monocot","gymnosperm","fern"],"ex":"These are all eudicot traits; eudicot pollen also has three openings, while monocot pollen has one. Roses, beans and sunflowers are eudicots.","img":null},"HW36":{"subject":"Signals & hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q15 (etiolation)","q":"What is etiolation?","ok":"Growth adapted to darkness: pale, long stems with small unexpanded leaves and short roots","wrong":["Greening of a shoot after it is exposed to light","Bending of a shoot toward light","Leaves dropping in autumn"],"ex":"A potato sprouting in a dark cellar is etiolated. It puts its energy into stem elongation to reach light, and it makes no chlorophyll because there is no light to use.","img":null},"HW37":{"subject":"Signals & hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q15 (de-etiolation)","q":"What happens during de-etiolation (\"greening\")?","ok":"After light exposure, stem elongation slows, leaves expand, roots grow, and the shoot makes chlorophyll","wrong":["The shoot grows even longer and stays pale","The plant drops its leaves","The seed goes dormant"],"ex":"Light switches the shoot to normal growth. De-etiolation activates enzymes that work directly in photosynthesis, enzymes that supply chlorophyll precursors, and enzymes that change hormone levels.","img":["../drill/img/deetiolation.jpg","Signal transduction in de-etiolation: phytochrome, cGMP and Ca2+, protein kinases, transcription factors"]},"HW38":{"subject":"Signals & hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q15 (three steps)","q":"What are the three steps of a signal transduction pathway, in order?","ok":"Reception, transduction, response","wrong":["Transduction, reception, response","Response, reception, transduction","Reception, response, transduction"],"ex":"Reception: a receptor protein detects the signal. Transduction: relay proteins and second messengers carry and amplify it. Response: a cellular activity changes.","img":null},"HW39":{"subject":"Signals & hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q15 (reception)","q":"In de-etiolation, what is the receptor?","ok":"Phytochrome, a light-detecting protein","wrong":["cGMP","Auxin","A transcription factor"],"ex":"Receptors are proteins that change shape in response to a specific stimulus. In de-etiolation, the receptor is phytochrome in the cytoplasm.","img":["../drill/img/deetiolation.jpg","Signal transduction in de-etiolation: phytochrome, cGMP and Ca2+, protein kinases, transcription factors"]},"HW40":{"subject":"Signals & hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q15 (transduction)","q":"Which second messengers carry the signal in de-etiolation?","ok":"Ca<sup>2+</sup> ions and cyclic GMP (cGMP)","wrong":["ATP and NADPH","Auxin and ethylene","DNA and RNA"],"ex":"Activated phytochrome opens Ca<sup>2+</sup> channels, raising Ca<sup>2+</sup> in the cytosol, and activates an enzyme that makes cGMP. Both second messengers activate protein kinases.","img":["../drill/img/deetiolation.jpg","Signal transduction in de-etiolation: phytochrome, cGMP and Ca2+, protein kinases, transcription factors"]},"HW41":{"subject":"Signals & hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q15 (response)","q":"In the response step, how does a signal pathway change a cell's activity?","ok":"By post-translational modification of existing proteins (phosphorylation by kinases) or by transcriptional regulation (transcription factors turn genes on or off)","wrong":["By changing the DNA sequence of the cell","By destroying the receptor","Only by opening stomata"],"ex":"Protein kinases phosphorylate proteins to activate them, and phosphatases switch the pathway off. Transcription factors can be activators (more transcription) or repressors (less).","img":["../drill/img/deetiolation.jpg","Signal transduction in de-etiolation: phytochrome, cGMP and Ca2+, protein kinases, transcription factors"]},"HW42":{"subject":"Signals & hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q16 (bending)","q":"What causes a coleoptile to bend toward light?","ok":"Auxin moves to the shaded side, so cells there elongate faster than cells on the lit side","wrong":["Cells on the lit side elongate faster","Light kills cells on the shaded side","Cytokinin builds up on the lit side"],"ex":"Phototropism comes from unequal growth: more auxin on the shaded side means more elongation there, which tips the shoot toward the light.","img":null},"HW43":{"subject":"Signals & hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q16 (where the signal is made)","q":"Where is the phototropism signal produced?","ok":"In the tip of the coleoptile","wrong":["In the roots","In the seed","Along the whole stem equally"],"ex":"The Darwins found that a coleoptile bends only when its tip is lit: removing the tip or covering it with an opaque cap stops the bending, while shielding the bending region doesn't.","img":null},"HW44":{"subject":"Signals & hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q16 (which part responds)","q":"Which part of the coleoptile bends, and how does the signal get there?","ok":"The region below the tip; a mobile chemical moves down from the tip","wrong":["The tip itself; nerve-like electrical signals","The roots; signals through the xylem","The whole seedling at once; by light alone"],"ex":"Boysen-Jensen showed that the tip's signal passes through permeable gelatin but not impermeable mica, so it is a chemical. Went then collected it in agar blocks: auxin (IAA).","img":null},"HW45":{"subject":"Signals & hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q16 (acid growth hypothesis)","q":"Plant cells are surrounded by rigid walls. According to the acid growth hypothesis, how does auxin let them elongate?","ok":"Auxin activates proton pumps; H<sup>+</sup> pumped into the wall lowers its pH, activating expansins that loosen it, so water uptake can stretch the cell","wrong":["Auxin dissolves the cell wall completely","Auxin pumps H<sup>+</sup> into the cytoplasm, making the wall stiffer","Auxin triggers cell division instead of elongation"],"ex":"Low pH activates expansins and wall-loosening enzymes that cleave cross-linking polysaccharides. Cellulose microfibrils then slide apart, the vacuole takes up water, and turgor stretches the cell.","img":["../drill/img/acid-growth.jpg","The acid growth hypothesis: proton pumps acidify the cell wall, expansins loosen it, and the cell elongates"]},"HW46":{"subject":"Signals & hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q17","q":"Which is an example of a plant hormone whose effect depends on its concentration?","ok":"Auxin: low concentrations stimulate stem cell elongation, while high concentrations inhibit it","wrong":["Abscisic acid: low levels close stomata and high levels open them","Gibberellin: low levels cause leaf fall and high levels cause dwarfism","Ethylene: it works only at one fixed concentration"],"ex":"Plant responses depend on how much of a hormone is present. Auxin is the classic example: very high levels inhibit elongation, partly by triggering ethylene production. The ethylene triple response also gets stronger as ethylene concentration rises.","img":null},"HW47":{"subject":"Signals & hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q18 (main role)","q":"What do cytokinins do?","ok":"Stimulate cell division (cytokinesis), working with auxin to control cell division and differentiation","wrong":["Close stomata during drought","Ripen fruit","Keep seeds dormant"],"ex":"Cytokinins are made in actively growing tissues (roots, embryos and fruits). The ratio of cytokinin to auxin steers what a group of cells becomes.","img":null},"HW48":{"subject":"Signals & hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q18 (apical dominance)","q":"How do cytokinins affect apical dominance?","ok":"They promote growth of axillary (lateral) buds, opposing the suppression from the terminal bud","wrong":["They strengthen the terminal bud's suppression of axillary buds","They have no role in branching","They cause the terminal bud to fall off"],"ex":"Cytokinins, auxin and strigolactone interact to control apical dominance. Remove the terminal bud (the source of auxin) and the axillary buds grow, making the plant bushier.","img":null},"HW49":{"subject":"Signals & hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q18 (anti-aging)","q":"How do cytokinins slow the aging of some plant organs?","ok":"By inhibiting protein breakdown, stimulating RNA and protein synthesis, and drawing nutrients in from nearby tissues","wrong":["By triggering a burst of ethylene","By closing the stomata","By breaking down chlorophyll"],"ex":"That's why florists use cytokinin sprays to keep cut flowers fresh. Ethylene does the opposite: it promotes senescence.","img":null},"HW50":{"subject":"Signals & hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q19","q":"What is the role of gibberellins?","ok":"Stem elongation, fruit growth and seed germination","wrong":["Closing stomata and keeping seeds dormant","Leaf abscission and fruit ripening","Defense against insect herbivores"],"ex":"Gibberellins stimulate growth of stems and leaves, help seeds break dormancy and use stored reserves, and with auxin let fruit set. Growers spray them on Thompson seedless grapes to get larger grapes.","img":null},"HW51":{"subject":"Signals & hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q20","q":"Which molecule is responsible for fruit ripening and the abscission (falling) of leaves?","ok":"Ethylene","wrong":["Cytokinin","Gibberellin","Brassinosteroids"],"ex":"A burst of ethylene triggers ripening. A shift in the balance of auxin and ethylene controls leaf abscission in autumn. Ethylene also drives the triple response and senescence.","img":null},"HW52":{"subject":"Signals & hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q21","q":"Brassinosteroids are similar to which hormones?","ok":"The steroid sex hormones of animals (chemically similar to cholesterol)","wrong":["Animal insulin","Ethylene gas","Abscisic acid"],"ex":"Brassinosteroids are steroids, chemically similar to cholesterol and to animal sex hormones. In plants their effects resemble auxin's: they induce cell elongation and division.","img":null},"HW53":{"subject":"Signals & hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q22 (roles)","q":"What are the major roles of abscisic acid (ABA)?","ok":"Slowing growth, keeping seeds dormant, and helping plants tolerate drought by closing stomata","wrong":["Ripening fruit and dropping leaves","Speeding up seed germination","Causing stems to grow taller"],"ex":"ABA is the main internal signal for drought: when it accumulates, stomata close rapidly. It also keeps seeds dormant until conditions are good.","img":null},"HW54":{"subject":"Signals & hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q22 (breaking dormancy)","q":"How is ABA-based seed dormancy broken in some seeds?","ok":"ABA is removed by heavy rain, light, or prolonged cold","wrong":["ABA levels rise sharply","The seed is buried deeper","Ethylene is washed out of the seed"],"ex":"Removing ABA lets the seed germinate when conditions are near optimal. Seeds with low or inactive ABA germinate too early (precocious germination), as in red mangroves and some maize mutants.","img":null},"HW55":{"subject":"Signals & hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q23 (thigmomorphogenesis)","q":"How can mechanical stress change a plant's form?","ok":"Thigmomorphogenesis: plants that are rubbed or shaken grow shorter and stockier","wrong":["They grow taller and thinner","They switch to making only roots","They stop photosynthesizing"],"ex":"Rubbing the stems of young plants a couple of times a day makes them shorter than controls. Ethylene, produced under mechanical stress, is part of the cause.","img":null},"HW56":{"subject":"Signals & hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q23 (triple response)","q":"A seedling growing upward hits a stone. Ethylene triggers the triple response. What are its three parts?","ok":"Slower stem elongation, a thicker stem, and horizontal growth","wrong":["Faster elongation, a thinner stem, and downward growth","Leaf fall, fruit ripening, and flowering","Stomatal closure, dormancy, and wilting"],"ex":"The triple response lets a shoot grow around an obstacle. Ethylene-insensitive (<i>ein</i>) mutants don't show it.","img":null},"HW57":{"subject":"Signals & hormones","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q23 (touch responses)","q":"Which is an example of a plant responding to touch?","ok":"Tendrils curling around a support (thigmotropism), or <i>Mimosa</i> leaflets folding when touched","wrong":["Shoots growing upward against gravity","Seeds germinating after red light","Leaves dropping in autumn"],"ex":"Thigmotropism is directional growth in response to touch, common in vines. <i>Mimosa pudica</i> folds rapidly through turgor changes triggered by electrical impulses (action potentials).","img":null},"HW58":{"subject":"Light & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q24 (Pr and Pfr)","q":"Phytochrome exists in two forms. How does light switch between them?","ok":"Red light converts Pr to Pfr (the active form); far-red light converts Pfr back to Pr","wrong":["Far-red light converts Pr to Pfr; red light converts Pfr to Pr","Blue light converts Pr to Pfr; UV converts it back","Darkness converts Pr to Pfr instantly"],"ex":"Pfr triggers seed germination, inhibits vertical growth and stimulates branching, sets internal clocks, and controls flowering. In the dark, Pfr slowly reverts to Pr in some species.","img":null},"HW59":{"subject":"Light & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q24 (final flash)","q":"Lettuce seeds get flashes of red, then far-red, then red light. What happens?","ok":"They germinate, because the last flash was red","wrong":["They don't germinate, because far-red light was included","The result can't be predicted","They germinate only if the first flash was red"],"ex":"Red and far-red effects are reversible, so the last exposure decides. It is red here, leaving phytochrome mostly as Pfr, which promotes germination.","img":null},"HW60":{"subject":"Light & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q24 (sunlight)","q":"Why does sunlight trigger germination even though it contains both red and far-red light?","ok":"Pr turns into Pfr faster than Pfr turns back, so sunlight raises the Pfr:Pr ratio","wrong":["Sunlight contains no far-red light","Sunlight destroys Pr","Phytochrome ignores sunlight"],"ex":"Because conversion to Pfr is faster, full sun builds up Pfr. Under a canopy, leaves filter out red light, so far-red dominates and more phytochrome is Pr.","img":null},"HW61":{"subject":"Light & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q25 (short-day)","q":"When do short-day plants flower?","ok":"When the night is longer than a critical night length (usually late summer, fall or winter)","wrong":["When the night is shorter than a critical night length","Only after a flash of red light at night","At any time of year, regardless of night length"],"ex":"Short-day plants are really long-night plants. The critical night length sets the minimum number of hours of darkness they need to flower.","img":null},"HW62":{"subject":"Light & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q25 (long-day)","q":"When do long-day plants flower?","ok":"When the night is shorter than a critical night length (usually late spring or early summer)","wrong":["When the night is longer than a critical night length","Only after a cold treatment","Only in complete darkness"],"ex":"For long-day (short-night) plants, the critical night length sets the maximum number of hours of darkness. A flash of red light that breaks up a long night can make them flower.","img":null},"HW63":{"subject":"Light & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q26","q":"Cyclic changes in plants over a roughly 24-hour period, such as flowers opening and closing, are called","ok":"circadian rhythms","wrong":["photoperiodism","phototropism","thigmomorphogenesis"],"ex":"Circadian means about a day. Circadian rhythms continue without outside cues, last about 24 hours, can be reset (entrained), and compensate for temperature. Photoperiodism is the response to day and night length across the seasons.","img":null},"HW64":{"subject":"Light & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q27 (stress)","q":"What does \"stress\" mean for a plant?","ok":"Any environmental condition that can harm its growth, development or productivity","wrong":["Only attack by herbivores","Only lack of sunlight","The normal daily circadian rhythm"],"ex":"Stress can be abiotic (drought, flooding, salt, heat, cold) or biotic (pathogens and herbivores). Plants cope through physiological, chemical and developmental responses.","img":null},"HW65":{"subject":"Light & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q27 (drought)","q":"How does a plant cope with drought?","ok":"It makes ABA, which closes stomata to reduce water loss","wrong":["It forms air tubes in its roots","It makes heat-shock proteins","It makes antifreeze proteins"],"ex":"ABA is the main drought signal. Closing stomata cuts transpiration; leaves may also roll or wilt to reduce their exposed surface.","img":null},"HW66":{"subject":"Light & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q27 (flooding)","q":"How does a plant cope with flooding?","ok":"It forms air tubes in the roots that help them survive low oxygen","wrong":["It closes stomata with ABA","It makes antifreeze proteins","It stores salt in its leaves"],"ex":"Waterlogged soil has little oxygen. Ethylene triggers controlled cell death in the root cortex, which opens air channels (aerenchyma) that carry oxygen to submerged roots. That's why \"flooding → ABA in the shoots\" is the wrong pairing in a Ch 39 clicker question.","img":null},"HW67":{"subject":"Light & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q27 (salt)","q":"How does a plant cope with salty soil?","ok":"It makes solutes it can tolerate at high concentrations, so it doesn't lose water by osmosis","wrong":["It forms air tubes in its roots","It makes heat-shock proteins","It opens its stomata wider"],"ex":"Salty soil pulls water out of roots by osmosis. Producing compatible solutes raises the cells' own solute concentration, so water stays in.","img":null},"HW68":{"subject":"Light & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q27 (heat)","q":"How does a plant cope with excessive heat?","ok":"It makes heat-shock proteins that help keep other proteins from denaturing","wrong":["It makes antifreeze proteins","It forms air tubes in its roots","It drops all its roots"],"ex":"Heat-shock proteins act as chaperones that help other proteins hold their shape at high temperatures.","img":null},"HW69":{"subject":"Light & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q27 (cold)","q":"How does a plant cope with cold?","ok":"It adjusts membrane fluidity, avoids osmotic water loss, and makes antifreeze proteins against ice crystals","wrong":["It makes heat-shock proteins","It forms air tubes in the roots","It closes its flowers"],"ex":"Cold stiffens membranes, so plants change their lipid makeup to keep them fluid. Antifreeze proteins block ice crystals from forming and damaging cells.","img":null},"HW70":{"subject":"Light & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q28 (defenses)","q":"Which list names plant defenses against pathogens?","ok":"The epidermis and periderm as a barrier, the hypersensitive response, and systemic acquired resistance","wrong":["Antibodies and phagocytic white blood cells","Fever and inflammation","Making more gibberellins to outgrow the pathogen"],"ex":"The first line of defense is the physical barrier of the epidermis and periderm. Pathogens that get in through wounds or stomata trigger chemical defenses. Plants don't make antibodies, which belong to the vertebrate adaptive immune system.","img":null},"HW71":{"subject":"Light & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q28 (hypersensitive response)","q":"What does the hypersensitive response do?","ok":"It kills cells near the infection site, makes enzymes that attack the pathogen, and changes cell walls to confine it","wrong":["It spreads the pathogen to the rest of the plant","It makes antibodies against the pathogen","It makes the plant grow faster"],"ex":"By killing its own cells around the infection, the plant walls off the pathogen and leaves visible lesions (see the infected tobacco leaf on the slides).","img":null},"HW72":{"subject":"Light & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q28 (salicylic acid)","q":"What is the role of salicylic acid in plant defense?","ok":"It triggers systemic acquired resistance, so the whole plant can respond quickly to a later infection","wrong":["It kills the pathogen directly at the wound","It closes the stomata during drought","It ripens fruit"],"ex":"Methylsalicylic acid made around the infection site travels through the phloem. Healthy distant cells convert it to salicylic acid, which switches on defense genes (PR proteins).","img":null},"HW73":{"subject":"Light & defense","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q28 (herbivores)","q":"How can a maize plant defend itself against caterpillars that are eating it?","ok":"Chemicals in the caterpillar's saliva trigger it to release volatile compounds that attract parasitoid wasps, which lay their eggs in the caterpillars","wrong":["It makes antibodies against the caterpillar","It closes its stomata","It drops its leaves"],"ex":"Plants also use thorns and distasteful or toxic secondary compounds, and some \"recruit\" predators, like the wasps here, that attack the herbivore.","img":null},"HW74":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q36 (what lichens are)","q":"What are lichens?","ok":"A symbiotic association between a fungus and a photosynthetic microbe (a green alga or a cyanobacterium)","wrong":["A kind of moss","A single fungal species","A plant growing on a fungus as a parasite"],"ex":"A lichen looks like one organism but is two (or more) partners, often among the first life on bare rock.","img":null},"HW75":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q36 (the fungus's role)","q":"What does the fungus contribute to a lichen?","ok":"The body and shape: it absorbs water and minerals, protects the partner, and secretes acids that break down rock","wrong":["Sugars from photosynthesis","Fixed nitrogen in every lichen","Chlorophyll"],"ex":"The photosynthetic partner provides carbon compounds (sugars), and cyanobacteria also fix nitrogen. The fungus provides the structure, water and minerals.","img":null},"HW76":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q37","q":"Why are fungi important in the biosphere?","ok":"They decompose dead matter and recycle nutrients, form mutualisms (mycorrhizae, lichens), and some are pathogens","wrong":["They are the main producers in most ecosystems","They fix most of the nitrogen on Earth","They are the main food of large animals"],"ex":"Fungi break down even cellulose and lignin and return carbon, nitrogen and minerals to the ecosystem. Most land plants depend on mycorrhizal fungi. Fungi also give us food, antibiotics (penicillin) and model organisms (yeast).","img":null},"HW77":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q38","q":"What are fruiting bodies, such as mushrooms?","ok":"Reproductive structures made of tightly packed hyphae that produce and release spores","wrong":["The fungus's feeding organs","Single giant cells","Fruits that the fungus steals from plants"],"ex":"Most of a fungus is the hidden mycelium. The fruiting body is the short-lived, spore-making part above ground: basidiocarps (mushrooms) in basidiomycetes, ascocarps in ascomycetes.","img":null},"HW78":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q39 (types)","q":"What types of reproduction occur in fungi?","ok":"Both sexual and asexual; both usually produce spores","wrong":["Only sexual reproduction","Only asexual reproduction","Only binary fission"],"ex":"Asexual spores form by mitosis (e.g., conidia, or budding in yeasts). Sexual spores form after the fusion of mating partners and meiosis.","img":null},"HW79":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q39 (sexual sequence)","q":"What is the order of events in fungal sexual reproduction?","ok":"Plasmogamy (cytoplasm fuses) → heterokaryotic stage → karyogamy (nuclei fuse) → meiosis → haploid spores","wrong":["Karyogamy → plasmogamy → meiosis → spores","Meiosis → plasmogamy → karyogamy → spores","Mitosis → karyogamy → plasmogamy → spores"],"ex":"In fungi, the cytoplasm and the nuclei fuse at different times, sometimes years apart. Meiosis follows the fusion of nuclei, which is the answer to the Ch 31 clicker question.","img":null},"HW80":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q40","q":"What are hyphae and the mycelium?","ok":"Hyphae are tiny tube-like filaments; the mycelium is the interwoven mass of hyphae that forms the fungus's body","wrong":["Hyphae are spores; the mycelium is the fruiting body","Hyphae are roots; the mycelium is the stem","Hyphae are nuclei; the mycelium is the cell wall"],"ex":"Hyphae have chitin walls. They are either septate (divided by cross-walls with pores) or coenocytic (no cross-walls, many nuclei). The mycelium's huge surface area makes it efficient at absorbing food.","img":null},"HW81":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q41","q":"How do fungi get their nutrients?","ok":"They are heterotrophs that absorb food: they secrete enzymes into their surroundings to break down molecules, then absorb the small products","wrong":["By photosynthesis","By swallowing food and digesting it inside the body","By chemosynthesis from rocks"],"ex":"Fungi digest food outside their bodies with exoenzymes, then absorb it. They can be decomposers, parasites (some use haustoria to take nutrients from hosts) or mutualists.","img":null},"HW82":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q42 (zygomycetes)","q":"What happens in the life cycle of a zygomycete such as black bread mold?","ok":"Hyphae of opposite mating types fuse to form a tough zygosporangium, which undergoes karyogamy and meiosis and releases spores when conditions improve","wrong":["Eight ascospores form in a sac","Basidiospores form on gills","Flagellated spores swim in water"],"ex":"The zygosporangium is resistant to freezing and drying. Zygomycetes also reproduce asexually from sporangia on upright hyphae, which is the black fuzz on moldy bread.","img":null},"HW83":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q42 (ascomycetes)","q":"What happens in the life cycle of an ascomycete?","ok":"After karyogamy and meiosis, spores form inside a sac (ascus), usually eight ascospores in a row, within an ascocarp; asexually they make conidia","wrong":["A dikaryotic mushroom forms basidia on its gills","Mating types fuse into a zygosporangium","Flagellated zoospores swim to new hosts"],"ex":"Asci are packed into a fruiting body called an ascocarp. Eight ascospores in a row means an ascomycete (a Ch 31 clicker question).","img":null},"HW84":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q42 (basidiomycetes)","q":"What happens in the life cycle of a basidiomycete (a mushroom)?","ok":"A long-lived dikaryotic mycelium forms a basidiocarp; karyogamy and meiosis in club-shaped basidia on the gills make basidiospores","wrong":["Eight spores form in a sac","Mating types fuse into a zygosporangium","Spores have flagella"],"ex":"Most of the life cycle is dikaryotic: each cell has two different haploid nuclei. The mushroom you eat is made of dikaryotic cells (a Ch 31 clicker question).","img":null},"HW85":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q43","q":"What is special about chytrids?","ok":"They have flagellated spores (zoospores), the only fungi that do, which suggests they resemble the earliest fungi","wrong":["They have no cell walls","They form most mushrooms","They form mycorrhizae with most plants"],"ex":"Chytrids live in lakes and soil and have chitin walls. One chytrid is killing amphibians worldwide.","img":null},"HW86":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q44 (zygomycetes)","q":"Which feature is special to zygomycetes?","ok":"Resistant zygosporangia; they are fast-growing molds such as black bread mold (<i>Rhizopus</i>)","wrong":["Asci with eight spores","Club-shaped basidia","Flagellated spores"],"ex":"Zygomycetes have mostly coenocytic hyphae. They are the fuzzy black mold you throw away before your parents visit.","img":null},"HW87":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q44 (ascomycetes)","q":"Which feature is special to ascomycetes (sac fungi)?","ok":"Spores made in sacs (asci), often within an ascocarp; they include yeasts, morels, truffles and <i>Penicillium</i>, and are the fungal partner in most lichens","wrong":["Club-shaped basidia on gills","Resistant zygosporangia","Flagellated zoospores"],"ex":"Ascomycetes range from single-celled yeasts to cup fungi. Asexually they make conidia at the tips of hyphae.","img":null},"HW88":{"subject":"Fungi","src":"Homework 3 (professor's questions, answers written from the slides)","tag":"HW3 · Q44 (basidiomycetes)","q":"Which feature is special to basidiomycetes (club fungi)?","ok":"Club-shaped basidia and a long-lived dikaryotic mycelium; they include mushrooms, shelf fungi, rusts and smuts, and are key decomposers of wood","wrong":["Spores in sacs (asci)","Resistant zygosporangia","Flagellated zoospores"],"ex":"Basidiomycetes are good at breaking down lignin. A fairy ring is the edge of one basidiomycete mycelium growing outward.","img":null}}}};
